>people with any breach of skin integrity (for example, cuts, burns, blisters or skin infections) >people who misuse drugs intravenously >people with indwelling lines or catheters. -Sepsis risk stratification and assessment in people aged 18 or over (Nice guideline) or tongue) Behaviour Respiratory Heart rate Blood pressure Green (low risk) Normal No high risk or moderate to high risk None of or red signs Amber (moderate risk) from patient, friend or relative of new-onset altered behaviour or mental history Of acute deterioration Of functional ability
- Trauma, surgery or invasive procedures in
- 21—24 breaths per minute
- 91—130 per minute for pregnant women, 100—130 beats per minute
- New-onset arrhythmia Systolic blood pressure 91—100 mmHg Red (High risk) Pale. mottled. ashen. blue Objective evidence Of altered behaviour or mental state
- all 25 breaths per minute or more
- new need for 40% oxygen or more to maintain saturation more than 92% (or more than in known chronic Obstructive pulmonary disease)
- more than 130 per minute
- 90 mmHgor less OR more than 40 mmHg below normal Not passed urine in the past 12—18 hours, Not passed urine in previous 18 hours, or for catheterised patients passed 0.5—1 or for catheterised patients passed less ml/kg Of urine per hour than OS mukg of urine per hour Tympanic temperature less than WC Signs of potential infection:
- Non-blanching rash
- Redness
- swelling or discharge at surgical site
- Cyanosis Of Skin. lips or tongue -Management in out of hospital (healthcare community, school, workplace, aged 18 years and over —Green light: discharge with appropriate advice

—Amber light: >Can definitive condition diagnosed and treated in out of hospital setting ? yes! Then Treat definitive condition access medical care and/or provide information to safety net. not! Urgent referral to emergency care (which has resuscitation facility) —Red light: Urgent referral to emergency care (which has resuscitation facility) -Management of Pt with green and amber risk factors in hospital setting:pegple aged 18 years and over Low risk Suspected sepsis, no high or high to moderate risk criteria Manage according to clinical judgment Moderate risk 2 or more moderate to high risk criteria met OR systolic blood pressure Of 91- 100 mmHg send tests blood gas lactate blood culture full blood C-reactive protein urea and electrolytes creatinine Lactate over 2 mmol/L Manage as high risk Only I moderate to high risk criteria met Consider blood test in I hr and clinician review Lactate 2mmol-less, no AKI Can definitive condition be diagnosed and treated? Yes! Manage definitive condition. If appropriate, discharge With information definitive condition Sn
- t diagnosed then consider antibiotic and senior decision maker review within 3 hrs

Management of Pt with High-risk factors in hospital setting:people aged 18 years and over
- 1 or more high risk criteria met
- Arrange immediate review by senior clinical decision maker (emergency care ST4 or above) Carry out venous blood tests for the following:
- blood gas for glucose and lactate
- blood culture
- full blood count
-
- C-reactive protein
- urea and electrolytes
- . creatinine
-
- clotting screen Give intravenous antibiotics without delay (within a maximum of 1 hour) Discuss with consultant Lactate over 4 mmol/L Give IV fluid bolus 500ml without delay and discuss with critical care unit Lactate 2-4 mmol/L Give IV fluid bolus 500ml without delay Lactate< 2 mmol/L Consider IV fluid bolus 500m! without delay Continuous monitoring in emergency department Consultant review if Pt condition isn't improved

