    'hemorrhagic-shock-massive-transfusion',
    'Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Hemorrhagic Shock & Massive Transfusion

Class of shock Volume of blood loss (mi) Volume of blood loss (%) Heart rate Blood pressure Pulse pressure Respiratory rate Mental state Class I Up to 750 0-15% <100 Normal Normal or increased 14-20 Slightly anxious Class II 750-1500 15-30% >100 Normal Decreased 20-30 Mildly anxious Class Ill 1500-2000 30-40% >120 Decreased Decreased 30-40 Class IV >2000 >140 Decreased Decreased Confused, Anxious, confused lethargic —Management of hemorrhagic shock -In the ED, priorities are: 1 restore intravascular volume 2 maintain oxygen-carrying capacity so that tissue oxygen delivery meets demand Z limit ongoing blood loss 4_ Prevent the development of coagulopathy and hypothermia >Follow ABC approach

>lf spontaneous ventilation is not adequate, intubate and ventilate to achieve an arterial hemoglobin oxygen saturation of 294%. -Vascular access and monitoring >Establish two large bore 14-16 guage peripheral lines or intraosseous/central line if peripheral problematic. >Draw blood sample for cross match , FBC, blood gas analysis , toxicology study and pregnancy testing for all female of childbearing age. —Initial fluid replacement >Administer IL bolus of isotonic fluid in adult and 20ml/kg for paediatric Pt >Observe the Pt response to initial fluid administration and base further therapeutic and diagnostic decision on this response. >Early resuscitation with blood and blood product should be considered in Pt with evidence of class Ill and IV hemorrhage. >urine output is one of the prime indicator of fluid resuscitation and Pt response. >Adequate resuscitation should produce 0.5mI/kg/hr urine out in adult and Iml/kg/hr in children or 2ml/kg/hr in infant. >Base deficit or lactate level can be useful to determine the presence and severity of the shock —Packed red blood cells (blood replacement) >Those Pts who doesn't or little response to initial fluid therapy, they require pRBC, plasma and platelet as an early part of their resuscitation.

>W'hen possible, typed and cross-matched blood is preferable. However, if time and the patient's clinical status do not permit full crossmatching, type-specific blood is the next option, followed by low-titer O-negative blood. >Rh-ve pRBC are preferred for female of child bearing age >Some Pt needs massive transfusion. It defines as unit pRBC within 24 hours or >4 units in I hour. —Coagulopathy >severe hemorrhage leads to consumption of coagulation factor and early coagulopathy. >The administration of RBC units without additional clotting factors or platelets during massive transfusion results in further impairment of haemostasis from both haemodilution (dilutional coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia) and metabolic derangement (acidosis and hypocalcaemia from citrate in storage solution, and hypothermia from refrigeration). Acidosis and hypocalcaemia are detrimental to normal haemostasis. Furthermore, hypothermia is associated with impairment of both platelet and coagulation factor activity. >AII of these factor contributes to progressive coagulopathy. >Progressive coagulopathy can be prevented if other blood products should be added to massive resuscitation eg; FFP, Platelets in Pt with haemorrhagic shock. previous  Next

