  INSERT INTO "lessons" (
    "id", "module_id", "slug", "title", "type", "body", "is_free_preview", "position", "status", "created_at", "updated_at"
  ) VALUES (
    gen_random_uuid(),
    v_module_id,
    'left-anterior-vs-posterior-fascicular-block',
    'Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Left Anterior vs Posterior Fascicular Block

Left anterior fascicular block (LAFB) Left anterior/superiorfascicular block is due to to anatomical Of functional block in the anterior fascicle. Depolarization entirely depends on posterior fascicle. The initial vector will be directed inferiorly yieldinga small in inferior leads (II, Ill, aVF) and small q-wave lateral leads(aVL, l, aVR) The second vector which is considerably stronger be directed to the left, back and upwards. This result in deep S- wave in inferior leads and large R-wave in left lateral leads. Hence, inferior leadsrS complex and lateral leads shows qR complex (means small q-wave large-R wave). The electrical axis will be shifted to the left (left axis deviation) range between -450 and -900
- Causes of LAFB Cardiomyopathy, hyperkalemia, hypertension, MI , LVF, myocarditits -300 aVL AV node LAFB ECG Criteria Large-R wave LAFB The electrical axis will be shifted to the left (left axis deviation) range between -450 and -900. If the electrical axis is between -300 to -450 more likely to suggests LAFB QRS duration <0.12 seconds but slightly prolonged aVL show qR complex. V5-V6 usually also shows qR complexes. Left posterior fascicular block (LPFB) It's much less common Depolarization ofthe left ventricle depends entirely on impulses from the anterior fascicle if posterior one is defect. The vector initially directed upwards and to the left which yield q-wave in aVF and R-wave in lead in l. The second vector is directed downwards to the right, which results in prominent R- wave in lead aVF and equally prominentS-wave in lead l. The electrical axis will be more positive than 90' (right axis deviation). Causes of LAFB Cardiomyopathy, hyperkalemia, hypertension, MI , LVF, myocarditits -30 aVL 2 LPFB
- LPFB ECG Criteria Electrical axis +900 to +1800
- rs complexes in lead I and aVL qR complexes in inferior leads (II, Ill and aVF). Q —wave is mandatoryin leads Ill and aVF.

QHSdurauon<U.12 seconds Lead II, Ill, aVF displaysrS complexes.
- ECG morphology of LAFB
- qR complexes in lateral limb leads, and rs complexes in inferior leads
- Axis deviation

- Left Axis Deviation (LAD)
- Leads I and aVL are POSITIVE;
- Leads II, Ill and aVF are NEGATIVE
- Example of LAFB

- Left axis deviation
- qR complexes in I, aVL
- rScomplexes in II, Ill, aVF
- Prolonged R wave peak time in aVL
- Increased QRS voltage in limb leads
- ECG morphology of LPFB

VR 111
- As above, there are rs complexes in leads I and aVL, and qR complexes in leads II, Ill and aVF
- Axis deviation

- 111 Right Axis Deviation (RAD) Leads II, Ill and aVF are POSITIVE; Leads I and aVL are NEGATIVE Example of LPFB

VF previOUS

