    'Genetourinary Trauma',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Genetourinary Trauma

>motor vehicle crashes, falls, assaults and sports injuries are the rnost common mechanisms for blunt GU injuries, whereas gunshot wounds and stab wounds are the most common causes for penetrating injuries. >SeatbeIt use and air bag deployment have increasingly been associated with decreased rates of nephrectomy in renal trauma patients. -Renal trauma (kidney_jnjury) following-features-indicates-renaLinjury >Flank contusions or ecchymosis, >palpable mass, > lower rib fractures, >penetrating wounds in the flank -Clinical features: Pt will complain flank and loin pain followed by haematuria. :P.iagnosi.s; contrast-enhanced CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis is the imaging "gold standard" for the stable patient with suspected renal injury >RenaI angiography can identify renal vascular injury -Management : mostly conservatively (analgesia, bed rest, antibiotic -Bladder trauma >only 2%

>Associated with pelvic fracture >Suspect bladder injury in alcohol-intoxicated patients (bladder often distended) who are in a motor vehicle crash (potentially high-energy transfer resulting in pelvic fracture). -Clinical features; >Lower abdominal pain and tenderness and gross hematuria are commonly associated findings cystogram is the "gold standard" -Management: >Extraperitoneal rupture is most common and usually manage with catheter drainage >lntraperitoneal rupture always require surgical repair >ExtraperitoneaI ruptures may need surgical intervention in following situation . >Urinary catheter does not allow appropriate drainage >Associated rectal or vaginal injury >Associated bladder neck injury >Open fixation of a pelvic fracture (to avoid contamination of the hardware) >Pelvic fracture with bone spicules in bladder lumen -Urethral injury_

>The posterior urethra consists of prostatic and membranous portions. >Associated with rnotor vehicle collisions or falls from heights. >The anterior urethra is divided into the relatively fixed bulbar segment and the penile segment. >Associated with direct perineal trauma -Clinical features >Symptoms may include hematuria, dysuria, or inability to void. Rectal and perineal examination may demonstrate a perineal hematoma or high-riding prostate. >Posterior urethral injury is suggested by the triad of urinary retention, blood at the meatus, and a high-riding prostate >Following feature indicates consideration of suprapubic catheter and for bladder drainage and retrograde urethrogram for diagnosis of urethral injury so for that Pt urgently refer to urologist >high-riding prostate, >meatal blood, >perineal ecchymosis, >scrotal hematoma, >pelvic fracture, or gross hematuria, >any resistance to gentle passage of a Foley catheter

- Management >FoIIow ATLS protocol >Male PT with posterior urethral injury secondary to pelvic fracture need immediate placement of suprapubic catheter for bladder drainage with delay surgical repair >Most penetrating injuries to the anterior urethra require surgical exploration and repair. —Note: scrotal and testicular trauma (scrotal or testicular hematoma) ,manage conservatively with good analgesia however testicular rupture need surgical intervention. >Fracture of penis refer urgently to urologist for surgical exploration, evacuation of haematoma and repair. previous  Next

