  -- Lesson: Maxilofascial Trauma
  INSERT INTO "lessons" (
    "id", "module_id", "slug", "title", "type", "body", "is_free_preview", "position", "status", "created_at", "updated_at"
  ) VALUES (
    gen_random_uuid(),
    v_module_id,
    'maxilofascial-trauma',
    'Maxilofascial Trauma',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Maxilofascial Trauma

>lf the patient has got the missing tooth with them, further questions to ask are: >What medium was the tooth kept in (ideal medium is saliva or milk)? >ls the patient aware if this is an adult or a baby tooth? > If-thepatjent-js-not-aware-where-the-mjssin following considerations should be thought_Qf; >if there are deep lacerations to the lips or cheeks, it is important to consider that the fragment may have become lodged in these areas. A soft tissue x-ray can help identify if there are any foreign bodies in the lips >lf there is a risk of the teeth and/or teeth fragments being aspirated, it is worth considering taking a chest x-ray to exclude this. As enamel is the most calcified structure in the human body, it often appears quite easily on an x-ray. -Investigation >CXR: Used to exclude aspiration risk when patient is unaware of where a missing tooth or tooth fragment is. >Soft tissue view: Useful to exclude fragments of teeth becoming lodged within deep lacerations to the lips, cheeks and face. >Orthopantomogram (OPT or OPG): Can be useful if doubt exists over whether tooth is a deciduous (baby) or adult tooth, and useful to assess degree of damage to dental and periodontal structures. -Management

- Immediate manaqement of avulsed tooth hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth. The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable medium (saliva or milk) before re-implantation. Primarv teeth manaqement If the primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldn't be re-implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth. f the primary teeth are very loose and present a potential airway risk, then they should be removed Adult tooth management Adult Luxated intruded and extruded teeth should be replaced into the correct position, and a splint(wire splint or silk suture splints) should be used to stabilize them and arrange follow-up with dentist. Avulsed adult teeth should be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth. saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried out. Tetanus booster should be consider and antibiotic (tetracycline or penicillin V) should be recommended patients advised to rinse with chlorhexidine 0.1% mouthwash for 1-2 weeks Eygomaticomaxillary Complex (ZMC) fractures >ZMC fractures, also known as tripod, tetrapod, quadripod, malar or trimalar fractures. >functionally it is more correct to consider it as having four legs, like a four-legged stool ponding-to-the-four-legs.-Q.f-the-stQQl-ar.e.2 >Lateral orbital rim >lnferior orbital rim >Zygomaticomaxillary buttress — the thickened area of bone forming the lateral wall of the maxillary sinus

>Zygomatic arch —MOI: blow to the side of the face from a fist, from an object, or secondary to motor vehicle accidents. -Initial assessment >Follow ABDE approach >The airway is of particular concern, either from direct injury or from complications of local trauma, bleeding, swelling or foreign body >Allowing a conscious patient to sit up may help maintain airway patency and reduce the risk of aspiration (only if no spinal injuries are present). >left lateral position may be required in Pt with spinal injury >Prompt haemostasis of external bleeding may involve direct pressure, sutures or staples. >lnternal haemorrhage control may require packing, balloon tamponade, temporary reduction of fractures, or either embolization or surgical ligation of bleeding vessels. >AII maxillofacial traurna patients should be considered high risk for cervical spine injuries. —On physical examination, flattening of the malar eminence will be noted in the absence of often significant swelling >The position of the eye and visual acuity r
- nust be checked. >Enophthalmos and proptosis both indicate a significant orbital injury. Features suggesting progressive retrobulbar haemorrhage require an emergency

decompression (lateral canthotomy). -Investigation; >Request Occipitomental 150 (OM15) or occipitomental 300 (OM30) views >SubmentoverticaI view >CT scan is far more accurate at identifying ZMC and zygomatic arch fractures, its use in the ED is restricted to either a second line investigation, usually initiated by the maxillofacial team ZManagement; >Most of ZMC fracture can be manage expectantly until local swelling subsides and and reviewed by a maxillofacial surgeon. >urgent referral to maxillofacial surgeon or ophthalmologist if Pt with reduced visual acuity or diplopia due to orbital involvement. >Advise Pt avoidance of nose blowing as this may produce surgical emphysema, not to occlude the nose when sneezing and application of ice packs to the area to reduce swelling to infraorbital nerve

e Eygomatic arch fractures >The zygomatic arch is predominantly formed by the zygomatic process of the temporal bone which articulates with the much smaller temporal process of the zygoma forming the arch. —MOI: Assault, motor vehicle or motorcycle accidents —Clinical features: Pt may have alteration in bite or difficulty moving the jaw. >Sensory disturbance to the cheek and upper gum due to infraorbital nerve injury. —Investigation: Request Submentovertical view or lateral facial view >FaciaI CT is needed to define the extent of the injury. —Management: mostly Pt are manage conservatively and follow-up

>Urgent referral to maxillofacial surgeon if Pt has restriction to mouth opening due to trapping of the temporalis muscle or mandibular condyle. >Advise Pt avoidance of nose blowing as this may produce surgical emphysema, not to occlude the nose when sneezing and application of ice packs to the area to reduce swelling Submentovertex view -Orbital blow out fracture

—MOI: Direct blow to the globe of the eye (commonly from a squash ball or a shuttlecock), resulting in a fracture of the orbital floor and prolapse of contents into the maxillary sinus. —Clinical features: Pt may have diplopia and can't look up and medially due to inferior rectus entrapment. >Test infra-orbital nerve function as infra-orbital nerve commonly damage in this injury. —Investigation: X-ray will reveal "tear drop sign", clouding of sinus and surgical emphysema. —Management: Urgently Refer all Pt with orbital blow-out fracture (e.g. diplopia or reduced eye movements) to a maxillofacial surgeon and/or an ophthalmologist >facial CT scan will be needed to visualise the fracture in detail and plan surgical repair.

- Nasal bone fracture >The nasal bones are easily fractured due to them being broad and flat, however, proximal nasal bone fracture may be accompanied by fractures of adjacent bones which may lead to CSF rhinorrhoea (due to involvement of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone) and orbital emphysema from fracture of the orbital plate. —Clinical features: Epistaxis, Septal haematoma, nasal discharge eg CSF rhinorrhoea and supraorbital or supratrochlear nerve paraesthesia. —Diagnosis: Nasal fracture is a clinical diagnosis and displaced fractures must be reduced before 14 days. —Management: referral to otolaryngologist if Pt has following features: >UncontroIIed haemorrhage, septal haematoma/ abscess or CSF rhinorrhoea >Refe.r.raLtQ-o.tQ.laryngQ.logist-wj.thinüzlQ-days.-Q.f-jnjury-if-e.t-wjth-fQ.llowj.ng features: obvious new deformity or new septal deviation. >0ther all manage conservatively and arrange otolaryngology follow-up within 7 to IO days. -Mid facial fractures/ Le Fort facial fractures -MOI: Midfacial fractures can be caused by motor vehicle crashes, sports, assault, and falls due to seizures or intoxication or in the elderly. >Le Fort fractures lie between the frontal bone, the skull base, and the mandible. They involve the upper jaw, teeth, nose, and maxillary and ethmoid air sinuses.

—Le Fort I is a transverse fracture separating the body of the maxilla from the pterygoid plate and nasal septum. Only the hard palate and teeth move, similar to a loose upper denture. —Le Fort II is a pyramidal fracture through the central maxilla and hard palate. Movement of the hard palate and nose occurs, but not the eyes. —Le Fort III is craniofacial dysjunction when the entire face is separated from the skull from fractures of the frontozygomatic suture line, across the orbit and through the base of the nose and ethmoids. the entire face shifts with the globes held in place only by the optic nerve. —Le Fort IV fracture includes characteristics of the Le Fort Ill and also involves the frontal bone. -Clinical features: >Pt often present with significant hemorrhage, early swelling, bilateral orbital ecchymosis, and cerebrospinal fluid leaks in Le Fort II and Ill injuries. —Investigation: CT scan of the face with coronal and axial slices with three- dimensional reconstructions. —Management: >Resuscitate and establish a clear airway: >Pt often present with significant haemorrhage, requiring nasal or oral packing. >Refer all patients with middle third or naso-ethmoidal fractures to the maxillofacial surgeons for admission.

>Discuss patients with CSF leaks with the neurosurgeons >CIean and dress compound facial lacerations and may need formal debridement and they provide access to fractures for open reduction and internal fixation.

- Mandibular fracture >Mandible fractures are the second rnost common facial fracture after nasal fractures. —MOI: Assaults, motor vehicle crashes and falls. > The mandible may be fractured at a site distant from the point of impact eg a fall on the chin may cause condylar fractures. —Clinical features: >Pain aggravated by jaw rnovement or biting. >Pt may present with swelling, tenderness and intraoral bruising. >Numbness of the lower lip indicates injury to the inferior dental nerve. —Investigation: >Request an orthopantomogram (OPG). —Management: >Manage undisplaced fractures not involving teeth with analgesia , soft diet, prophylaxis antibiotic (penicillin , co-amoxiclav), tetanus cover and arrange outpatient follow-up with maxillofacial surgeon. >Refer displaced or multiple fractures, bilateral condyle fractures or single fractures with malocclusion or deviation of the jaw on opening to on-call maxillofacial surgeon.

- Temporomandibular joint dislocation >Anterior dislocations are the rnost common, however, it can be uni- or bilateral. —MOI: Acute dislocations can be seen after trauma or dystonic reactions, but they are usually a result of extrerne mouth opening such as with yawning. —Clinical features: >Pain over the temporal fossa >Patient cannot close the mouth. >The jaw protrudes anteriorly, and difficulty in swallowing leads to drooling of saliva

—Investigation: obtain x-ray incase of direct traurna only. —Management: >Reduce the dislocated joint >After reduction, advise the patient to take a soft diet and not to yawn or open the mouth widely for 24hr previous  Next

