  -- Lesson: Burn Injury
  INSERT INTO "lessons" (
    "id", "module_id", "slug", "title", "type", "body", "is_free_preview", "position", "status", "created_at", "updated_at"
  ) VALUES (
    gen_random_uuid(),
    v_module_id,
    'burn-injury',
    'Burn Injury',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Burn Injury

>A burn is defined as a traumatic injury to the skin or other organic tissue primarily caused by thermal or other acute exposures >Children and adults are more vulnerable to burn injury. It is important to exclude NAI in children ly-Pes of burn: Thermal : This is the most common type of burn and includes flame burns, scalds (from hot liquids) and contact burns (from hot objects e.g. an iron or radiator). Acids and alkalis found in household chemical products can produce very deep burns through coagulative and liquefactive necrosis.4They will continue to burn the skin until completely removed. Electrical: As an electrical current travels through the body it creates an entry and exit point, damaging tissue along its path as it is converted from electrical to thermal energy. Cold exposure (frostbite)zThese burns are caused by ice crystals which can form both intra and extracellularly. Radiation;-Radio frequency energy or ionising radiation causes tissue damage. The most common type of radiation burn is sun burn. Pathophysiology_

Jackson's burn wound model
- Local response Zone of Coagulation: occurs at the point of maximum damage i.e. the nearest point to the heat source. There is irreversible tissue necrosis here due to coagulation of proteins. Zone of Stasis:surrounding the zone of coagulation, this area is characterised by decreased tissue perfusion. Burns resuscitation can help to increase tissue perfusion and prevent this damage from becoming irreversible. otherwise it could evolve into an area of necrosis. Zone of Hyperaemia: the outermost zone as its name suggests is where there is increased tissue perfusion. This is a reversible zone. Note: The release of cytokines and other inflammatory mediators at the site of injury has a systemic effect once the burn reaches 20-30% of total body surface area. Such as CVS changes (Capillary permeability is increased). Respiratory changes(bronchoconstriction). metabolic rate increases. Initial assessment >Need to follow trauma protocol such as primary or secondary survey

>Use ABCDE approach to the primary survey is critical to ensure that any life- threatening issues are not missed Airway -Following features that increase the suspicion of airway obstruction or inhalation injury
- Assessment Singed nasal/facial hairs Burns to the face/oropharynx Inflamed oropharynx Breathing
- Management Sit Pt upright Any suspected airway

Circulation & Fluid resuscitation -Assessment >Burns total body surface area (TBSA) in Adults and in children can cause profound circulatory shock that can occur from both large fluid losses through tissue damage and from a systemic inflammatory response >Burns <15% in adults and in children do not require immediate fluid resuscitation. >Capillary refill time (CRT), blood pressure and mucous membrane assessment are important indicators of hydration status. >early catheterisation is important as urine output is a reliable sign that can demonstrate poor perfusion and serve as a guide to ongoing resuscitation.

>ln circumferential limb burns, blood supply to the extremities should be checked regularly. >ln a significant burn there can be an increased metabolic demand on the patient which can cause organ dysfunction therefore: >blood tests (FBC, U&E, C-reactive Protein, LFTs and capillary blood glucose) should be send. -Management -Fluid resuscitation: >AduIts with burns >15% TBSA and children with burns TBSA require fluid resuscitation. >Get IV access, If it's not possible to get IV or IO access through unburnt skin then place the IV in burnt skin. -use parkland formula (2-4mI x weight in kg x %TBSA). >AduIt: 2-ml (Hartman or plasma-lyte) x weight in kg x %TBSA >Paeds 3-ml x weight in kg x %TBSA >the total resuscitation fluid required over 24 hours from the time of the burn. One half of fluid should be given in first 8hrs and remaining half in 16 hours >Note: Urine output targets should be doubled if rhabdomyolysis is suspected -Send blood tests

>Creatine kinase is a useful test to perform to assess muscle breakdown >EvaIuate any areas of circumferential burns in limbs and constantly reassess perfusion.any deterioration in the circulation to a limb could indicate ischaemia or a compartment syndrome. require urgent intervention such as escharotomy or fasciotomy. Disability-exposure >Full exposure to assess of size and depth of burns >maintain body temperature by both active and passive warming. >Clean the burn with normal saline and cover with strips of cling film -Analgesia: >Non-pharmacoIogical >cooling the burn under cold running water and covering with cling film. Cling film also helps to reduce heat loss and the risk of infection. and NSAIDs or opiods and ketamine Assessment of the burn (Secondary survey) _ -Assessment of burn Formulas/charts

Hoad and neck Anterior Lund and Browder chart Arm 9% -Assess depth of the burn Superficial epidermal burn: only epidermis affected . Skin is red and painful but not blistered eg: sun burn

Superficial partial thickness : burn extend to superficial layer. Skin is moist painfully hypersensitive and blanch to touch Deeæpartial thickness burn: Extend to deeper layer dermis. Skin is drier ,cherry red, less painful, blistered red or mottled appearance and doesn't blanch to touch Full thickness burn : extends through entire dermis to subcutaneous tissue skin appears waxy white, brown , painless , generally dry and doesn't blanch to pressure

Management of burn wound Chemical burn & management

Electrical burn & management

Referral to specialist burn center >AII burns 22% TBSA in children or 23% TBSA in adults >AII full thickness burns >AII circumferential burns >Any burn not healed in 2 weeks >Any burn with suspicion of non-accidental injury should be referred to a burn unit/centre for expert assessment within 24 hours >AII burns to hands, feet, face, perineum, or genitalia

>Any chemical, electrical or friction burn >Any cold injury >Any unwell/febrile child with a burn >Any concerns regarding burn injuries and co-morbidities(e.g. serious medical conditions (such cardiac dysfunction, diabetes, cancer or immunosuppression) that may affect treatment or healing of the burn Escharotomy >VVith full thickness and deep partial thickness burns, the dermis can become very stiff. If this occurs circumferentially over the chest it can restrict chest wall mover-nent and lead to mechanical respiratory failure. Equally if there are circumferential burns to the limbs this can cause limb ischaemia. In these cases an emergency escharotomy needs to be undertaken to release the rigid skin to allow: adequate ventilation (if the chest is involved) or circulation (in the limb). -Procedure: The limb should be kept in the anatomical position. >The area is cleaned and incised along the anatomical lines with a scalpel down to the fat > The incision should not go down to the muscle or fascia >For the limbs the incisions need to release both medial and lateral aspects >For the chest the incision needs to release the whole breast plate >Care must be taken to avoid the named important structures

previous  Next

