on CT. —Diagnostic imaging in thoracic or lumbosacral column injury only_(children and adults); >Perform an X-ray as the first-line investigation for people with suspected spinal column injury without abnormal neurological signs or symptoms in the thoracic or lumbosacral regions (TI—L3). >Perform CT if the X-ray is abnormal or there are clinical signs or symptoms of a spinal column injury. —Whole-body scanning; >Use whole-body CT (consisting of a vertex-to-toes scanogram followed by CT from vertex to mid-thigh) in adults (16 or over) with blunt major traunna and suspected multiple injuries. Patients should not be repositioned during whole-body CT. -The canadian C-spine rule algorithm

The Canadian C-Spine Rulel For alert Score 15) and trauma patients When spine injury is a Concern. Age Or Dangerous mechanism* or paresthesias in extremities ANY LOW RISK FACTOR THAT ALLOWS ASSESSMENT OF RANGE OF MOTION? Simple rear-end MVC" or Sitting position in ED or Ambulatory at any time or Of Or Absence Of midline cervical Spine tenderness V&ESD/j 45 degrees left and right II. Bt_ej NO RADIOGRAPHY
