cord injury: -methylprednisolone -nimodipine -naloxone. -Neurogenic vs Spinal shock
- Neurogenic shock Neurogenic shock occurs with CNS or spinal cord injury that
- Spinal shock probably occurs in less than 20% of spinal cord—injured patients Spinal shock is the temporary loss or Loss of peripheral sympathetic innervation results in extreme vasodilatation secondary to loss of sympathetic arterial tone. If the Tl through T4 cord levels are compromised, loss Of sympathetic innervation to the heart leaves unopposed vagal parasympathetic cardiac innervation eventually leads to bradycardia or an absence of reflex tachycardia. General features: Patients with neurogenic shock are warm, peripherally vasodilated, and hypotensive with a relative bradycardia. Management: Give Iv fluid along with vasopressors as Pt does not respond to IV fluid alone. Neurogenic shock usually presents with hypovolemia so moderate fluid resuscitation should be given Give atropine if Pt has significant bradycardia Myotome distribution Reflexes depression of spinal reflex activity that occurs below a complete or incomplete spinal cord Injury. General features: flaccidity, loss of reflexes, and loss Of voluntary movement

