  -- Lesson: Throacic Trauma (life Threatining Injury )
  INSERT INTO "lessons" (
    "id", "module_id", "slug", "title", "type", "body", "is_free_preview", "position", "status", "created_at", "updated_at"
  ) VALUES (
    gen_random_uuid(),
    v_module_id,
    'throacic-trauma-life-threatining-injury',
    'Throacic Trauma (life Threatining Injury )',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Throacic Trauma (life Threatining Injury )

- Tension Pneumothorax -Mechanism of injury; most common mechanical positive-pressure ventilation in Pt with visceral pleural injury -Others: in subclavian or internal jugular catheter insertion, occlusive four-sides dressing to traumatic chest defect (think hole in the chest). -Pathophysiologyætension pneumothorax develop when one way air leak from the lung or through the chest, as a result of that air will be trapped eventually collapsed the affected lungs. The mediastinum will displaced to opposite side decreasing venous return and cardiac output -Clinical features >Tachypnea, Tachycardia, hypotension, distended neck vein >Contralateral tracheal deviation >UniIateraI hyper-resonant on percussion and absence of breath sound >Cyanosis (late manifestation) -Diagnosis >Tension pneumothorax diagnosed clinically. Pt is not critical then go for CXR or eFAST/US :Management >Perform immediate needle decompression, usually in the fifth/fourth intercostal space at the anterior axillary line, followed by chest tube insertion.

>Due to variable thickness of chest the cannula/catheter can kink and cease to function.in this case, Finger thoracostomy is alternative >Tube thoracostomy is mandatory after needle or finger decompression of the chest.

Tension pneumothorax CXR :Qpen Pneumothorax >think 'hole in the chest'. It is also known as a communicating pneumothorax or sucking chest wound. >Prompt clinical inspection front and back is required; a small sucking chest wound is usually audible -Initial management >CIose the defect (hole) with any sterile occlusive dressing eg: plastic wrap, sheet of jolnet or Ashermann device >Tape dressing 3 sides to provide flutter valve effect. >Don't completely occlude/close the wound (risk of tension pneumothorax) until chest tube is in place -Further management >Place Chest tube remote from open wound(means not from the site of wound) >Definitive treatment is surgical repair (wound closure)

open pneumothorax CXR -Massive Haemothorax >A massive haemothorax is result from rapid accumulation of more than 1500 ml of blood or one-third of the patient's blood volume >Most commonly occurs due to penetrating trauma, however it also occurs due to blunt traur-na. -Clinical features >Shock >Absence of breath sound >DuIIness on percussion >Flat neck veins

>Contralateral shift of mediastinum may cause distended neck vein >Use CXR and FAST to confirm diagnosis -Management >lntravenous fluid resuscitation >Appropriate use of blood and blood products >lnsert a chest drain >VVhen appropriate blood from chest tube can be collected in a device suitable for autotransfusion. Massive Haemothorax CXR -ATLS Indication for Thoracotomy

- Thoracotomy indication in massive hemothorax >Prompt drainage of 1500 ml blood, or a third of the patient's circulating volume > greater than 200 ml/hr blood loss for 2-4 hrs >Continued need for blood transfusion -Thoracotomy indication in other injury >Cardiac arrest in minutes in blunt trauma >Cardiac arrest minutes in penetrating trauma >Decompression of cardiac tamponade >Persistent hypotension in both blunt and penetrating trauma -Cardiac tamponade >Cardiac tamponade is the compression of the heart due to accumulation of the fluid in the pericardial sac >Penetrating injury to chest, back or upper abdomen? Think tension pneumothorax, think massive haemothorax, think tamponade. >FAST has 95% sensitivity according to ATLS and use to exclude or confirm the cardiac tamponade with FAST >PEA is suggestive of cardiac tamponade, however other causes can be possible. >150 to 200 ml of blood in the pericardial sac enough to cause cardiac tamponade

>Cardiac tamponade can develop slowly or rapidly, require rapid diagnosis and treatment. -Clinical features -Classic triad >Hypotension >MuffIed heart sound >Distended neck vein, may not be if Pt in hemorrhagic shock >KussmuaI's sign -Management >Fluid resuscitation to increase pre-load >Thoracotomy by qualified surgeon >Pericardiocentesis can be performed when qualified surgeon isn't available previous  Next -9

