  -- Lesson: Throacic Trauma and Faial Chest
  INSERT INTO "lessons" (
    "id", "module_id", "slug", "title", "type", "body", "is_free_preview", "position", "status", "created_at", "updated_at"
  ) VALUES (
    gen_random_uuid(),
    v_module_id,
    'throacic-trauma-and-faial-chest',
    'Throacic Trauma and Faial Chest',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Throacic Trauma and Faial Chest

- Flail chest and pulmonary contusion(lung contusion) >Think free-floating segment of ribs or sternum). This is common and small flails may be missed clinically. >The multiple rib fractures are a potential source of significant haemorrhage >Considerable force is required to create a flail chest in young people bruise Of the lung cause by thoracic trauma. Blood and other fluids accumulate in the lung tissue >PuImonary contusion occurs with or without rib fracture or flail chest >Respiratory failure usually develop over time rather than occurring instantaneously. -Diagnosis >Chest pain, tachypnea, chest wall contusions, and hypoxia suggest underlying pulmonary contusion. >Areas of lung opacification on chest imaging(cxr) within 6 hours of blunt traurna are usually considered diagnostic of pulmonary contusion >CT is more sensitive than plain radiograpgh -Management >Maintain adequate ventilation

>Pain control via epidural analgesia if Cl then intercostal nerve block >Caution fluid resuscitation >lnsert a chest drain for associated pneumothorax and haemothorax. :Complicatjon; ARDS :MyocardiaI contusion or blunt cardiac injury_ >Think bruised heart, for-example cardiac chamber rupture, coronary artery dissection and valvular disruption -Investigation >ECG, a normal ECG effectively rules out myocardial contusion >Unexplained tachycardia, atrial and ventricular ectopic maybe a clue of myocardial contusion >Consider bedside echocardiogram and troponin for evidence of myocardial injury ZMa.nage.m.e.nt >Monitor the Pt for the first 24hrs if PT's ECG normal then don't need monitoring >Address Hypoxaemia, acidaemia, fluid status, low haemoglobin. —Traumatic Aortic injury >Traumatic aortic injury is rnost common cause of sudden death after vehicle collision usually chest strike with steering wheel or a motor vehicle crash at speeds greater than 30 mph (50 km/h), and fall from great height (IO ft or 3m)

—The proximal descending aorta is most commonly injured in blunt trauma because of the fixation of the vessels between the left subclavian artery and the ligamentum arteriosum. >The diagnosis of ascending aortic injury is rarely made since very few patients survive long enough to diagnosis and repair. >The more distal the injury, the better is the anticipated outcome, Although the subclavian artery is occasionally avulsed at its origin because of sudden deceleration, direct trauma to the distal artery with intimal damage and occlusion associated with fractures of the first rib or clavicle is more likely. -Clinical features >OccasionaI clinical clues include generalised hypertension, upper extremity hypertension in combination with weak or absent femoral pulses, and a harsh systolic murmur -CXR features >W'idened mediastinum—the most reliable sign >Fractures of the first and second ribs >ObIiteration of the aortic knob >Deviation of the trachea to the right >Presence of a pleural cap >Elevation and rightward shift of the right main stem bronchus

>Depression of the left main stem bronchus >ObIiteration of the space between the pulmonary artery and the aorta >Deviation of the nasogastric tube to the right
- gation-of-cho.ice -Management >ControI pain with analgesia >Control BP and HR at level systolic no more than 110mmHg and HR 80 bpm with esmolol or labetalol >Surgical repair or endovascular stenting by a qualified surgeon

Aortic injury CXR —Diaphragmatic injury >Think breach in the diaphragm. It is usually caused by penetrating rather than blunt injury and is easily missed clinically and radiologically (X-RAY or CT). >Blunt injury is three times more common on left hemidiaphragm, its due to the protective effect of the liver on the right hemidiaphragm. >Any diaphragmatic breach will not heal spontaneously it's due to pressure gradients between chest and abdomen. >Abdominal content herniation is a possibility and may be picked up years later. -Diagnosingßiaphragmaticinjury >Orogastric tube placement with evaluation to see if the tube curves up from the abdomen into the chest >upper Cl series looking for displacement of viscera into the chest >CT of the chest and abdomen with contrast -Management >Surgically repair diaphragmatic injury >Operation for other abdominal injury often revealed a diaphragmatic injury

Diaphragmatic inj ur y CXR -Oesophageal injury. >Most commonly result from penetrating trauma, usually a ballistic injury. >BIunt trauma to esophagus is rare, however it does occurs due to forceful expulsion of gastric content (eg: extreme vomiting or injury) from the upper abdomen into esophagus which produce linear tear in the lower esophagus allowing content leakage into the mediastinum eventually leads to mediastinitis. >EsophageaI rupture secondary to vomiting called Boerhaave's syndrome. -Clinical features

>Chest or mid-epigastric pain >Pleuritic chest pain worsened by neck flexion and swallowing >Mediastinal crunch >Rapid development of sepsis >Pt may have left pneumothorax & hemothorax without rib fracture >Presence of mediastinal air on CXR suggest esophageal rupture which can be confirmed by contrast study or oesophagoscopy -Management >Esophageal rupture has high mortality >Prompt surgical repair along with plural space and mediastinal drainage required. >lv antibiotics

Oesophageal ur y X—ray -Tracheobronchial Tree injury_ >lt's an injury to trachea or bronchi, however its rare, typically caused by impressive deceleration injuries and most patients die at the scene of the accident. >Majority of tracheobronchial injury occurs within I inch of carina. >lntubation can cause injury or worsen an injury to trachea or proximal bronchi -Clinical features

>Haemoptysis >pneumomediastinum >Cervical subcutaneous emphysema (surgical emphysema, can be found in x-ray) >Tension pneumothorax >Cyanosis -Diagnosis : bronchoscopy confirm the diagnosis -Management >Obtain immediate surgical consultation >Discuss intubation strategy with senior anaesthetic colleagues, consider single or double cuffed tubes with use of fiber optics technique >lnsert two large bore chest drains to counter the air leak of a tracheo-bronchial injury

Tracheobronchial Tree injury X—ray -Simple pneumothorax >Pneumothorax results from air entering the potential space between the visceral and parietal pleura >Both penetrating and non penetrating traur-na cause the injury. >Lung laceration with air leakage is the r-nost common cause of pneumothorax from blunt traurna

—Note: Medium-term/long-term, patients with traumatic pneumothorax may wish to fly. No guideline regarding the safe timing of this exists. -Diagnosis >lnspect the chest for bruising, laceration and contusion >Assess the chest wall movement and compare breath sound bilaterally >Hyperresonance on percussion >An upright expiratory CXR aid for diagnosis -Nanagement >Chest tube insertion at the level of fifth intercostal space anterior to mid-axillary line >Observe and aspiration of small pneumothorax maybe appropriate previous  Next

