  -- Lesson: Abdominal Trauma
  INSERT INTO "lessons" (
    "id", "module_id", "slug", "title", "type", "body", "is_free_preview", "position", "status", "created_at", "updated_at"
  ) VALUES (
    gen_random_uuid(),
    v_module_id,
    'abdominal-trauma',
    'Abdominal Trauma',
    'article',
    $BODY$# Abdominal Trauma

>ln the UK, this is most commonly knife- related injury penetrating trauma, the injuries sustained are caused by direct injury as the penetrating item passes through the organs and tissues in its path. Stab wounds and Iow-energy gunshot wounds cause tissue damage by lacerating and tearing. High- energy gunshot wounds transfer more kinetic energy, causing increased damage surrounding the track of the missile due to temporary cavitation -Commonly affected organs >Liver 40% >Small bowel 30% >Diaphragm 20% >colon 15% >Gunshot injury >SmaII bowel 50% >colon 40% >Liver 30% -Wound assessment >Caution is required in assessrnent of penetrating abdominal wounds. Patients with penetrating injury, even with normal vital signs, should be assessed by a senior surgeon.

>Do not remove any object remaining in the wound, such as a knife, as this may be tamponading underlying vessels -Blunt abdominal trauma > The spleen and liver are the rnost commonly injured intraperitoneal organs in blunt trauma. >Blunt abdominal trauma commonly results from either a compression force, a deceleration injury or after direct impact with a car steering wheel . It has greater mortality than penetrating as there is often injury to multiple organ systems -Clinical features -Physical examination >lnspection — of the injured abdomen may show evidence of bruising, abrasions or penetrating trauma. >PaIpate- the abdomen in all quadrants, making note of general or regional tenderness and signs of acute abdomen (rigidity, rebound, guarding). Regardless of imaging, document serial exams for patients who remain in the ED for several hours. >RectaI examination — is useful if it demonstrates bleeding or a high-riding prostate suggestive of a pelvic injury and involvement of the genitourinary tract. Anal tone and sensation should be tested >Top-to-toe examination — including a log-roll is imperative to ensure there are no other injuries. -Solid organ injury_

>Signs and symptoms of a solid organ injury are generally due to pain and blood loss. >Hepatic and splenic injuries are at high risk for hemorrhage in both penetrating and blunt abdominal trauma. > An y-blunt-abdominal-trauma- patient-with-diffuseperjtonjtis-or-who-is hemodynamically unstable should be taken urgently for laparotomy. -Splenic injury is graded according to CT findings and treatment is guided by grade: >Grad-1 Minor subcapsular tear or haematoma >Grad-2 Parenchymal injury not extending to the hilum >Grad-3 Major parenchymal injury involving vessels and hilum >Grad-4 Shattered spleen >Grade I or 2 injuries can usually be managed conservatively -Other injuries -Hollow viscus injury_ >Peritoneal contamination with bowel contents will produce peritonism. —Pancreatic injury >lnjury to the pancreas may cause pancreatitis, which may develop over days. —Diaphragmatic injury

>Diaphragmatic injuries are uncommon (0.8% to 5% of patients with thoracoabdominal injury). >Delayed diagnosis may lead to herniation or strangulation of abdominal contents through the diaphragmatic defect. -Diagnosis tools
- FAST/US indication Pt unstable hemodynamically with blunt trauma or penetrating trauma but without immediate laparotomy It is accurate, rapid, noninvasive, repeatable, and portable. Massive hemoperitoneum is quickly detected with a single view Of Morison's pouch
- Disadvantage: US compared to CT is the inability to identify the exact source of free intraperitoneal fluid. Cl is indication for laparotomy -Management CT CT with IV contrast is the noninvasive gold standard study for the evaluation of abdominal injury Pt should be hemodynamically stable
- Disadvantage: high cost longer time can miss some viscus or pancreatic injuries GI: hemodynamically unstable PT. uncooperative or allergic PT DPL indication in hemodynamically unstable Pt with blunt trauma or penetrating but without indication of laparotomy
- Disadvantage: Invasive and risk of procedure related injury
- Cl indication Of laparotomv. previous abdominal surgery or!iver cirrhosis

- Indication in blunt trauma
- Absolute: Anterior abdominal injury with hypotension Abdominal wall disruption peritonitis Free air under diaphragm on chest radiograph Positive FAST or DPL in hemodynamically unstable patient CT-diagnosed injury requiring surgery
- Relative Positive FAST or DPL in hemodynamically stable patient Solid visceral injury in stable patient Hemoperitoneum on CT without clear source
- Note: A definitive patient management plan should be formulated as early as possible, including early consultation by relevant speciality team. This may include surgeons, radiologists, anaesthetists, theatre staff, critical care and transfer teams -Indication of laparotomy in penetrating trauma

- Indication of laparotomy in penetratinq trauma
- Absolute Injury to abdomen, back, and flank with hypotension Abdominal tenderness GI evisceration High suspicion for transabdominal trajectory after gunshot wound CT-diagnosed injury requiring surgery
- Relative Positive local wound exploration after stab wound previous

