    $BODY$# Acute Mesenteric & Ischemic Bowel Disease

Definition Risk factors Clinical feature Investigations Acute mesenteric ischaemia This is an umbrella term covering a number Of conditions, includingacute mesenteric arterial embolus and thrombus, mesenteric venous thrombus and non- occlusive mesenteric ischaemia (NOMI). They all have the features of impaired blood transfusion to the intestine Age>50 years History of smoking Hypercoagulable states (strong risk factor for mesenteric venous thrombosis)
- Afib, MI, structural heart disease History of vasculitis Of proportion. there may be minimal or no tenderness. later sta s- typical symptoms of peritonism develop, with rebound guarding and tenderness Urgent CT contrast/angiogram FBC (elevated WCC) ABGs & Serum lactate (metabolic acidosis and elevated lactate)
- LFTS ECG
- ECHO Chronic mesenteric ischaemia This is a chronic atherosclerotic disease of the vessels supplying the intestine. It is also known as intestinalangina. Usually all three major mesenteric arteries are involved Common in female age b/w 50 and

#### Clinical Scenario 70:
Generally caused by factors predisposi ng to atherosclerosis - eg, smoking, hypertension, diabetes mellitus and hyperlipidaemia. weight loss, postprandial pain ('intestinal angina') and a fear of eating nausea, vomiting, or bowel irregularity. Examination reveal - vague abdominal tenderness disproportionateto the severity of the pain
- FBC,LFTs Arteriography (gold standard) Mesenteric duplex ultrasonography Ischaemic colitis This is caused by a compromise of the blood circulation supplying the colon. Ischaemic colitis may also be caused by venous occlusion. The average age for diagnosis is

#### Clinical Scenario 70:
Thrombosis- Inferior mesenteric artery thrombosis. Emboli - Mesenteric arterial emboli. Strangulated hernia or volvulus. Vasculitis Hypercoagulable states acute-onset abdominal pain - most frequently located in the left iliac fossa. Tenderness to palpation over the affected bowel Nausea and vomiting loose motion containingdark blood (late stage) Urgent CT contrast/angiogram
- FBC (elevated WCC) ABCs & Serum lactate (metabolic acidosis and elevated lactate)
- LFTS
- AXR Colonoscopy may show blue, swollen mucosa Barium enema shows 'thumb orintinø' in the earlv nhase that

- Medical thera Start IV fluid and antibiotics NG tube Should be sited Surgical intervention Urgent laparotomy should be done for patients with peritonitis. Management The goals of surgery include re- establishmentof the blood supply to the ischaemic bowel; resection of all non-viable regions and preservation of all viablebowel. Endovascular revascularisation procedures may have a role with partial arterial occlusion.  Asymptomatic patients Managed conservatively, with smoking cessation and antiplatelettherapy. Symptomatic chronic mesenteric ischaemia open or endovascular revascularisation, Postoperative complication Renal failure Next may last for several days Medical therapy Start IV fluid and antibiotics The ischaemia may be transient and resolve once the cause of the hypoperfusion has been alleviated Surgical intervention Segmental colectomy Patients who have an acute episode of colonic ischaemia that evolves intoa segmental colitis pattern with symptoms persisting for >2 weeks, or who develop a protein-losing colonopathyor Recurrent sepsis in a patient who has symptomatically recovered from an acute episode Of colonic ischaemia

