    $BODY$# Gallstones, Cholecystitis & Biliary Colic

- Blood tests- LFTs, FBC
- Abdominal U/S
- MRCP if IJ/S has not detected common bile duct stones, but the bile duct is dilated and/or
- Liver function test results are abnormal.
- Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) if MRCP does not allow a diagnosis to be made.
- Management n in n rm I r n n rm I ili ry—tre.e.;-
- Reassure the patients that they do not need treatment unless they develop symptoms:
- Asymptomatic gallstones are found in the common bile duct,
- Offer referral for bile duct clearance and laparoscopic cholecystectomy
- Management of symptomatic gallstones
- pain relief
- Severe pain -Diclofenac 75 mg 1M repeat second dose 75mg after 30 minutes if necessary.
- For intermittent mild to moderate pain, offer paracetamol or NSAID, for example, diclofenac which can be given orally or rectally if nausea is a problem.
- Urgent-emergency-admission
- If patient systemically unwell with a suspected complication of gallstone disease,such as acute cholecystitis, cholangitis, or pancreatitis.
- Refer — all other people diagnosed with symptomatic gallstone disease to a surgeon to consider laparoscopic cholecystectomy.

- Complications
- Biliary colic (most common) Acute cholangitis
- Acute cholecystitis
- Acute pancreatitis
- Obstructive jaundice
- Fistula formation
-
- Biliary peritonitis. Gallbladder mucocele.
- Gallbladder cancer
-

Clinical features Biliary colic It occurs when gallstone transiently obstruct either the cystic duct or Hartmann's pouch pain -occurs in the epigastrium or right upper quadrant, radiate to right back or shoulder and typically lasts for more than 30 minutes, but less than eight hours. Often severe, and may be associated with nausea & vomiting. Pain often worse after having a fatty meal. Abdominal UIS LFTs (liver enzyme in normal range and no jaundice) MRCP if UIS has not Acute cholecystitis Acute cholecystitis is an inflammation of the gallbladder It usually occurs when a gallstone completely obstructs the gallbladder neck or cystic duct. Pain - Sudden-onset, constant, severe pain in the upper right quadrant, lasting several hours (>12 hours). Fever/chills Nausea & vomiting Exan
- ination - Right upper quadrant mass & tenderness Presence of Murphy's sign (inspiration is inhibited by pain on palpitation when the examiner's hand is positioned along the costal margin). Jaundice — occurs in up to 10% of patients Abdominal UIS Blood test FBC (elevated WCC & CRP) LFTs ( normal/ mild elevated liver enzymes) Serum amylase Investigations detected common bile duct stones, but the bile duct is dilated and/or Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) if MRCP does not allow a diagnosis to be made. Acute cholangitis The most common causes of acute cholangitis are common bile duct stones and bile duct obstruction caused by gallstones. Charcottriad: Jaundice - (total bilirubin234 pmol/L [22mg/dL]) Fever - >380C with or without chills. Pain - right upper quadrant or epigastric pain. Reynold's pentad: It includes charcot's triad + altered mental status and hypotension secondary to septic shock. FBC (WBC count x 1000/pL or WBC count x 1000/pL) C-reactive protein (CRP)217 mmol/L [21 mg/dL]) LFTs (liver enzymes are elevated) Transabdominal ultrasonography Abdominal CT

Follow symptomatic Management gallstone management protocol Admission Give IV antibiotics and analgesia Surgical assessment for cholecystectomy. previOUS  Next Admission Start IV antibiotic and fluid biliarv decompression biliary decompression is endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatogra phy (ERCP), with or without sphincterotomy and drainage stenting.

