    'article',
    $BODY$# Small & Large Bowel Obstruction

Large bowel obstruction mechanical interruption (either complete or partial) to the flow of intestinal contents, with multiple potential causes (e.g., malignant colorectal disease, colonic volvulus, benign stricture) >Approximately 60% of large Clinical presentations Investigations Small bowel obstruction At's a medical emergencies requiring early diagnosis and intervention Antra-abdominal adhesion is the most common cause of SBO. >Other- hemias, Crohn's disease, intestinal malignancy,appendicitis >Clinical features >Abdominal pain, >bloating, vomiting (possibly biliary), >rectal flatus and cessation of defecation. >abdominal tenderness >computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen and pelvis >Plain x-rav features >dilation of the small bowel (>3cm diameter) >more prominent valvulae con niventes creating a 'coiled- spring appearance >small bowel usually lies more centrally >Blood tests - FBC, >Serurn amylase & lipase Fluid resuscitation, >bowel decompression (using Ileus >lleus is a slowing of gastrointestinal motility accompanied by distention, in the absence Of a mechanical intestinal obstruction. At is a diagnosis of exclusion bowel obstructions are caused by after bowel obstruction has an underlying colorectal malignancy. >Siqmoid volvulus is more usually seen in frail or older patients. >Caecal volvulus is more rare and more commonly seen in younger patients. >Clinical features Antermittent abdominal pain >abdominal distention > Nausea & vomiting >recent weight loss hectal bleeding, palpable rectal mass & tympanic abdomen. tenesmus >urgent computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen and pelvis to be performed as soon as possible. > Plain x-ray >dilation of colon>6cm >dilation Of cecum > 9cm > The small bowel usually lies more centrally, with the large bowel framing it (lateral). >Sigmoid volvulus: a characteristic 'coffee bean' appearance. >Caecal volvulus: often described as having a fetal appearance (comma' shaped), Medical emergencies requiring early diagnosis and intervention. been ruled Out. At usually occurs in response to physiological stress, including surgery (usually gastrointestinal), sepsis, metabolic derangements, and gastrointestinal diseases. Aleus is commonly seen in the postoperative setting. >Vomiting >Abdominal distension Anability to tolerate oral feeding >Absence of flatus. >FBC A-J&E >abdomen and pelvis CT >ABGs >Postoperative ileus Ail by mouth and Antravenous fluki

and analgesia >Surgical consultation — as patient need urgent surgical intervention. Management Abdominal x-rays Example- 1 Irnuany- supporuve management Consider bowel decompression via nasogastric tube. Consider urgent flexible sigmoidoscopy in volvulus - Often endoscopic decompression using flexible sigmoidoscopy allows the twist in the bowel to be untwisted and the obstruction to be relieved. If decompression fail then refer to surgery. Refer to colorectal surgeon for emergency surgery — if patient has malignant obstruction or has developed peritonitis or perforation. Y-reduction in opioid analgesia ± replacement with non- opioid analgesia (to avoid narcotic Ileus). >Consider nasogastric decompression. >Nil by mouth and intravenous fluids >management of the underlying condition (acute/systemic illnesses associated with intestinal hypomotility such as diabetes mellitus, Chagas disease, scleroderma, and neurological diseases, Sepsis) reduce opioids, anticholinergics. Consider NG

- ray Abdominal x-ray of small bowel obstruction

Example - 2

Abdominal x-ray of sigmoid volvulus (large bowel obstruction) Example - 3

Abdominal X-ray of a Caecal volvulus (large bowel obstruction). previous  Next 9

