- Investigations x-rays of neck, chest, abdomen
- Blood tests- FBC, LJ&E, PT/INR Consider CT of neck, chest and/or abdomen
- Consider MRI
- Consider laryngoscopy, endoscopy
-
- Management Unstable patient management
- Patients should be considered unstable if they present with airway compromise,
- drooling, inability to tolerate fluids, evidence of sepsis, perforation, or active bleeding. Resuscitations
- Follow ABCD protocol and resuscitate appropriately
- Surgery
- Emergency surgery is mandatory in patients with perforation due to obstruction.
- Resect the perforated segment.

- consider NG/OG — If patient has trouble handling secretions (drooling and/or spitting)
- Consider IV antibiotic - if patient has sign of sepsis.
- Stablepatient-management Stable patients: oropharyngeal foreign body Stable patients: esophageal or rectal foreign body (excluding multiple magnets and batteries) Stable patients: esophageal or rectal foreign body - multiple magnets Stable patients: esophageal or rectal foreign body - batteries
- Complications 1st line Watchful waiting and serial radiographic follow-up 80% of ingested or inserted foreign bodies pass through the gastrointestinal (GI) tract without symptoms 2nd line
- fiberoptic nasopharyngoscopy - should be considered for patients with an object in the oropharynx that could not be seen during the physical exam. Foreign bodies can be removed using tongue depressor and laryngoscopy
- 1" line Watchful waiting and serial radiographic follow-up Endoscopy is required - for foreign bodies that are nonprogressive, sharp, or nonradiopaque, for multiple foreign bodies, for foreign bodies in the stomach >2.5 cm in diameter or >6 cm long, or for oddly shaped foreign bodies such as open safety pins. 2nd line Foley catheter removal - is a widely used technique for the removal of a single, smooth, blunt, radiopaque foreign body from the esophagus or rectum.
- 3'd line Surgery - For failed extraction or object of >IO cm in rectum. line Emergency endoscopic or surgical removal Multiple magnets beyond reach of endoscope - urgent surgical consultation 1st line Emergency endoscopic or surgical removal if impacted Batteries beyond reach of endoscope - watchful waiting + radiology
