Diverticulosis > Asymptomatic, At may present with a large, painless rectal bleed Clinical features >FBC, CRP, >Faecal occult blood test Investigations >Kidney function test >Endoscopy, colonoscopy, CT colonography to confirm the presence of diverticula. Advise >the condition is asymptomatic and no specific treatments are Diverticular disease Antermittent abdominal pain in the left lower quadrant (pain may be triggered by eating and may be relieved by the passage of stool or flatus). >Other symptoms >Bloating, constipation, diarrhoea, nausea. nectal bleeding. > Fever. > Dysuria Same >Arrange urgent admission a person with confirmed diverticular Diverticulitis >Constant abdominal pain (which is usually severe and starts in the hypogastrium before localizing in the left lower quadrant). >Sudden change in bowel habit and significant rectal bleeding or passage of mucus from the rectum. >Tenderness in the left lower quadrant, a palpable abdominal mass or distention on abdominal examination Same >Referral pathway >Urgent refer to surgery if patient has acute complicated diverticuliY

needed. >Eat a healthy balanced diet including whole grains, fruit and vegetables. Management >drink adequate fluid >Consider offering bulk- forming laxatives for people with constipation disease has significant rectal bleeding (for example, if the person is haemodynamically unstable), as urgent blood transfusion may be required. Af a person is symptomatic but does not need admission: >Advise Increase fiber diet and fluid intake >Quit smoking, weight loss >Avoid NSAID & Opiod >Offer Paracetamol for pain >Offer Bulk-forming laxatives if a high-fibre diet is unacceptable to the person. not prescribe antibiotics is (for example with peritonitis and sepsis), and for people who do not improve with medical treatment. >Offer an oral antibiotic if the person with acute diverticulitis is systemically unwell. but does not meet the criteria for referral. >Prescribe co-amoxiclav  mg tds for 5 days cefalexin 500 mg BD/tds + metronidazole 400 mg tds for 5 days. >For people with acute diverticulitis who are systemically well: >Offer paracetamol for pain >Advise lifestyle diet modification ( increase fiber diet & fluid intake).

