- Management of hypoxaemia and circulatory failure is key to the management of drowning.
- Check an arterial blood gas on all patients with a history of submersion, as a surprising degree of hypoxia may be present in an asymptomatic individual.
- Patients who are asymptomatic and have no evidence of respiratory compromise (no chest x-ray changes or hypoxia on ABC) after 6 hours can be safely discharged home. All symptomatic patients should be admitted for observation.
- Remember to look for evidence of precipitating causes and associated injuries and manage them appropriately.
- Submersion minutes is a good prognostic factor and minutes is a poor prognostic factor.
- Consider child abuse in young children who are drowned in bath tubs, buckets or shallow water.
- There is no good evidence to support the routine use of prophylactic antibiotics. The incidence of ventilator-associated pneumonia can be 34-52%, and treatment should be initiated as per local protocol. Treatment should ideally be guided by culture results. 23 / 90

