- Patient clinical features suggestive of cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (CVST) Cerebral venous thrombosis (CVT)
- It is rare and accounts for 0.5% of all strokes
- It occurs more frequently in young adults and children. 2,3 It is three times more common in females than in males.
- The principle pathology of CVT is thrombosis of cerebral veins and the commonest site of origin is believed to be the junction of cerebral veins and larger sinuses.
- Once a thrombus is formed in the cerebral or cortical veins, its extension can occlude large draining venous sinuses.
- Thrombophilias - — Factor V Leiden Acquired — eg antiphospholipid syndrome
- Infection - Intracranial Regional eg ear, nose, throat, head, neck Systemic
- Female - specific risk factors are more important in younger age groups (ie oestrogencontaining contraceptives, pregnancy and puerperium/postpartum - is the 6—8-week period from the time of delivery of the placenta up to the resumption of normal ovulation).
- Malignancy — intracranial malignancy is more common in older age group
- Inflammatory - Vasculitis eg Behqet's disease Systemic lupus erythematosus Inflammatory bowel disease Sarcoidosis. https
