- Diarrhea — antibiotic associated
- Disruption of the bowel microbiota and mucosal integrity. Overgrowth of toxin-producing strains of Clostridium (Clostridioides)
- difficile (a Gram-positive, spore-forming, anaerobic bacillus) due to disruption of harmless bacteria in the gut:
- Risk factors Age more than 65 years Underlying morbidity — Abdominal surgery, CKD, immunosuppression.
- Prolonged hospitalization or residence in a nursing home
- Antibiotics that have been frequently associated with
- difficileinfection include clindamycin,
- cephalosporins (especially third and fourth generation),
- fluoroquinolones,
-

- broad-spectrum penicillins.
- Other drugs_(acid suppression)
- PPI and H—z-receptor antagonists
- Investigations
- FBC
- Send stool sample to test for difficile infection
- Management
- Manage fluid loss and symptoms as for acute gastroenteriti*
- Avoid the use of antimotility drugs (such as loperamide) to treat diarrhoeal symptoms.
- NICE recommends:
- For the initial episode of mild-to-moderate difficile infection: vancomycin 125mg orally four times a day for IO days.
- Second-line antibiotic advice for a first epsiode of mild-to-moderate difficile infection: fidaxomicin 200mg orally twice a day for IO days.
- Advise
- Wash hand frequently with soap water
- Note that alcohol-based hand rubs are not effective in removing difficilespores.

- The diarrhoea should resolve in 1—2 weeks. It is not usually possible to determine whether antibiotic treatment is effective until day 7.
- They should not return to work or school until they have been free from diarrhoea for 48 hours. If medication is prescribed, they should ensure that the full course is completed and there is no further diarrhoea or vomiting for 48 hours afterwards.

