- Summary of clinical features of peripheral and central vertigo

Clinical features postural stability Hearing loss or tinnitus Other neurological symptoms Nystagmus Head impulse test Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre Alternate cover test Peripheral Can walk, although may be unstable and may not wish to mobilise. Possible with some causes (for example Meniere's disease, labyrinthitis). NO
- Horizontal nystagmus with a torsional component that does not alter in direction when the gaze changes. Beats away from the affected side. Disappears with fixation of the gaze. Large amplitude nystagmus is usually only seen early in the course of Meniere's disease or vestibular neuronitis. May be positive with acute unilateral vestibular loss. In BPPV: latency of symptoms and nystagmus with fatiguability and habituation; severe vertigo. Normal Central Inability to stand up or walk even with the eyes open. Uncommon but may occur (for example stroke or intracranial tumour). Usually (for example cranial nerve dysfunction, visual disturbance, speech defects, dysarthria, weakness, sensory changes, memory loss, and gait ataxia). However, not all people with vertigo due to a stroke will have focal neurological signs.
- Direction-changing nystagmus on lateral gaze (right beating on right gaze, left beating on left gaze).
- Purely vertical or torsional. Not suppressed by visual fixation. Non-fatiguable.
- Commonly large amplitude nystagmus. Negative, indicating a normal vestibulo-ocular reflex.
