#### Clinical Scenario 2:
A 25-year-old male with no known PMH or drug history presents to the ED with history of recurrent priapism usually resolve spontaneously but this time acute attack took longer time. Local anesthetic lidocaine given. What would be your next step of management?
- **A)** Aspirate blood with needle
- **B)** Ice compression
- **C)** C. Corpora cavernosa injection with phenylephrine
- **D)** Irrigation therapy only
- **E)** Physical therapy Explanation
- Each acute episode of recurrent priapism manages as ischaemic priapism
- After local anesthesia Insert wide bore butterfly through the glans into the corpora cavernosa and aspirate cavernosal blood until bright red arterial blood is obtained.
- Ischemic priapism management algorithm

- Initial conservative measures > Local anaesthesia of the penis Insert wide bore butterfly through the glans into the corpora cavernosa Aspiration cavernosal blood until bright red arterial blood is obtained
- Cavernosal irrigation Irrigate with 0.9% w/v saline solution
- Intracavernosal therapy inject intracavernosal adrenoceptor agonist Current first-line therapy is phenylephrine with aliquots of 200 pg being injected every 3-5 minutes until detumescence is achieved (Maximum dose of phenylephrine is Img within I hour) Surgical therapy Surgical shunting Consider primary penile prosthesis implantation if priapism has been present for more than 36 hours
- Stuttering_(Recurrent or Intermittent) priapism: management algorithm

- Manage each acute episode similar to that for ischaemic priapism
- Prophylaxis/prevention of future episode Hormonal therapies (mainly gonadotropin receptor hormone agonists or antagonists) and/or antiandrogens Initiate treatment with phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors (PDE51s) only when the penis is in its flaccid state Use digoxin, a-adrenergic agonists, baclofen, gabapentin or terbutaline only in patients with very frequent and uncontrolled relapses. Use intracavernous self-injections at home of sympathomimetic drugs for the treatment of acute episodes on an interim basis until ischaemic priapism has been alleviated.

