#### Clinical Scenario 8:
A 26-year-old male came to the ED after being punched several times on his face. his X-ray is given. Which of the following complications patient will likely develop?
- **A)** Numbness over the chin
- **B)** Forehead numbness
- **C)** Sensory loss in lower teeth
- **D)** Loss of sensation of inferior nasal septum
- **E)** Numbness in the lower lip Explanation
- X-ray shows a depression of the left orbital floor in keeping with an undisplaced fracture and a pathognomoniic "teardrop sign" is present. Additional minimally displaced fracture seen at the lateral wall of the left maxillary sinus with an air-fluid level in the left maxillary sinus.
- Infra-orbital nerve commonly injured in orbital blow out fractures. it innervates lower eyelid, upper lip and nose.
- Orbital blow out fracture
- MOI: Direct blow to the globe of the eye (commonly from a squash ball or a shuttlecock), resulting in a fracture of the orbital floor and prolapse of contents into the maxillary sinus.
- Clinical features: Pt may have diplopia and can't look up and medially due to inferior rectus entrapment.
- Test infra-orbital nerve function as infra-orbital nerve commonly damage in this injury.

- Investigation: X-ray will reveal "tear drop sign", clouding of sinus and surgical emphysema.
- Management: Urgently Refer all Pt with orbital blow-out fracture (e.g. diplopia or reduced eye movements) to a maxillofacial surgeon and/or an ophthalmologist
- facial CT scan will be needed to visualise the fracture in detail and plan surgical repair. Normal orbital floor' Teardrop

