- The appropriate management of an avulsed tooth within the first 30 minutes and an organized treatment plan are essential to increase tooth survival.
- hold the tooth at the crown only and avoid touching the root of tooth.
- The tooth should be held in a physiologically acceptable isotonic medium (saliva or milk, saline) before re-implantation.
- Primary teeth management
- If the primary tooth has been avulsed then it shouldntbe re- implanted. Doing this is likely to result in further damage to the underlying adult tooth.
- f the primary teeth are very loose and presenta potential airway risk, then they should be removed
- Adult tooth management should be replaced into the correct position, and a splint(wire splint or silk Suture splints) should be used to stabilize them and arrange follow-up with dentist. Awbed_teethshould be picked up from the crown only, and saline used to briefly clean debris from the root of the tooth; saline should also be used to irrigate the socket to remove any blood clots that may be present here
- The tooth should be gently placed back into the socket, and splinting carried Out for up to 2 week.
- Tetanus booster should be consider and antibiotic (tetracycline or penicillin V) should be recommended
- patients advised to rinse with chlorhexidine 0.1% mouthwash for 1-2 weeks

#### Clinical Scenario 6:
A 30-year-old male presented to the ED with 15 hours history of painful swelling of his left leg. On examination, you note his Calf muscle is red, hot and tender. His observations are: Heart rate 90bpm, BP 129/67mmHg 20 T 380C His leg image is given What is the most likely diagnosis?
- **A)** Acute cellulitis
- **B)** Erysipelas
- **C)** Erythema multiform
- **D)** Steven Johnson syndrome
- **E)** Necrotizing fasciitis
- Patient clinical features suggest acute cellulitis

- Acute cellulitis VS Necrotizing fasciitis

Acute cellulitis It is an acute bacterial infection of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue. Cellulitis most commonly affects the lower limbs, but Other areas, such as the upper limbs, face, ears, and trunk, can also be affected
- Causes Streptococcus pyogenes. Staphylococcus aureus Pseudomonas aeruginosa Vibrio vulnificus Aeromonas hydrophila etc
- Risk factors Trauma (due to a bite, burn, or laceration), Leg ulceration Maceration or fungal infection between the or a concomitant skin disorder (such as atopic eczema) Comorbidities (such as diabetes mellitus) lymphoedema and leg oedema
- Clinical features Red, painful, hot, swollen, and tender skin, that spreads rapid ly, Fever, malaise, nausea, shivering, and rigors On examination Diffuse redness or a well-demarcated edge Blisters and bullae may be seen There is usually an obvious skin break where the infecting organism may have entered, such as a wound.
- Investigations Investigations are not usually necessary but may be considered in certain cases. Swab for culture — if there is an open wound, penetrating injury. drainage. Ultrasonography — for distinguishing nonpurulent cellulitis from cellulitis With underlying abscess Skin biopsy WCC, ESR and CRP
- Management
- If cellulitis is diagnosed, categorize the severity using the Eron classification system, to help guide management decisions. (table is given below) Has Class III or IV cellulitis IS Very young (under 1 Of age) or frail, [s immunocompromized Has significant lymphoedema.
- Has facial cellulitis (unless Very mild).
- Has suspected orbital or periorbital cellulitis Necrotizing fasciitis It is a life-threatening subcutaneous soft-tissue infection that requires a high index of suspicion for diagnosis.
- Always Suspect necrotising fasciitis in a patient With a rapidly progressing soft-tissue infection and any of the following:
- Severe pain (disproportionate to the clinical findings) Anaesthesia over the site of infection
- Oedema and erythema (oedema will typically extend beyond the erythema) Systemic signs of infection
- Causes Type I necrotising fasciitis - is a polymicrobial infection such as Bacteroides or Peptostreptococcus or Enterobacterales or mon-group A streptococcus Type II necrotising fasciitis - is most commonly a monomicrobial infection with Streptococcus pyogenes (group A
- Risk factors Immunosuppression due to chronic illness (e.g., diabetes mellitus, alcohol dependence) Cutaneous trauma, Surgery, or ulcerative conditions Varicella zoster infections Intravenous drug use and hospitalization
- Clinical features Anaesthesia or severe pain over site of infection Fever Palpitations, tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypotension, and lightheadedness Nausea and vomiting Oedema or induration Vesicles or bullae Delirium
- Investigations Surgical exploration
- The 'finger test' is a surgical method that can be performed under local anaesthesia at the bedside for the diagnosis Of necrotising fasciitis. Blood and tissue culture, CRP, ESR
- Clotting SC reen Consider X-ray, MRI, ultrasonography
- Management Emergency surgical - Refer the patient immediately to the surgical team; necrotising fasciitis requires rapid debridement of all infected tissues in combination With empirical antibiotic therapy. MRSA antibiotic coverage - Continue vancomycin, linezolid, tedizolid, or daptomycin if MRSA is confirmed. Analgesia and fluid resuscitation

person has a penicillin allergy. prescribe either: Clarithromycin SOO mg twice daily for days, Or Doxycycline 200 mg on the first day then 100 mg once daily, for a total of 5—7 days. Erythromycin (in pregnancy) SOO mg four times daily for 5— 7 days.
- Note
- dose should be adjusted in children accordingly.
- Complications Necrotizing fasciitis Myositis Sepsis, Subcutaneous abscesses, Post-streptococcal nephritis.
- Lymphoedema, leg ulceration
- Eron classification of cellulitis Class I Class II Class II Class IV
- There are no signs of systemic toxicity and the person has no uncontrolled comorbidities. The person is either systemically unwell or systemically well but with a comorbidity (for example peripheral arterial disease, chronic venous insufficiency, or morbid obesity) which may complicate or delay resolution of infection. The person has significant systemic upset, such as acute confusion, tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypotension, or unstable comorbidities that may interfere with a response to treatment, or a limb- threatening infection due to vascular compromize. The person has sepsis or a severe life-threatening infection, such as necrotizing fasciitis.

#### Clinical Scenario 7:
A 28-year-old boxer presents to the emergency department after sustaining a punch to his ear during a match. On examination, his ear is swollen and

deformed. Image is shown. What is the most appropriate initial management?
- **A)** Immediate surgical drainage
- **B)** Apply ice and observe
- **C)** Refer to ENT specialist
- **D)** Prescribe oral antibiotics
- **E)** Aspirate and apply a tight bandage Explanation
- The first step in managing this condition is to aspirate the hematorna and apply a tight bandage to prevent re-accumulation. Referral to ENT may be necessary for follow-up, but initial aspiration is crucial.
- Acute auricular haematoma
- Auricular haematorna occurs following blunt trauma when blood and serum accumulate between the perichondrium and cartilage.
- most commonly affects the helical rim of the pinna but sometimes, can affect the conchal bowl.
- if left untreated a 'plate' of fibroneocartilage develops from the damaged perichondrium and the resulting scarring and regeneration of the cartilage leaves the cosmetically unsightly
- cauliflower ear', characterised by thickening and irregular projection of the ear.

- it is a relatively common condition in contact sports, particularly in rugby and boxing
- Management
- The first step in managing this condition is to aspirate the hematoma, however, it is complicated by re-accumulation which occurs unless adequate pressure can be maintained with packing or apply tight bandage.
- a more definitive result can be obtained by incision and drainage and either leaving a suction drain in situ or suturing a 'button' over the area to force apposition of the skin, perichondrium, and cartilage
- if treatment is delayed and a plate of fibroneocartilage has formed, then permanent ear deformity will result unless formal surgical excision is undertaken, which has been done up to 1 month following injury
- once a mature cauliflower ear has formed, then reconstructive plastic surgery is required to restore normal form to the pinna

#### Clinical Scenario 8:
A 55-year-old female with history of fall brought to the ED with complaint of severe right hip pain. On examination, right limb is shortened and internally rotated. His X-ray is given. What complication most likely patient develop in this injury?
- **A)** Sciatic nerve injury
- **B)** Femoral nerve injury
- **C)** Femoral artery injury
- **D)** Common peroneal nerve injury
- **E)** Obturator nerve injury Explanation
- X-ray shows posterior hip dislocation
- Cemp\igations-otpgsterior-hip.ßislogatign: Sciatic nerve injury, avascular necrosis of the femoral head and secondary OA
- Posterior hip dislocation
- Posterior hip dislocations results from a posterior force applied to a flexed knee, most commonly a dashboard injury.
- These injuries are associated with acetabular, femoral neck, and fernoral shaft fractures
- Clinical features: limb will be shortened and internally rotated, with the hip flexed and adducted.
- Check for sciatic nerve damage: exar-nine foot dorsiflexion and below- knee sensation
- Diagnosis: Order AP or lateral view radiograph Complications: Sciatic nerve injury, avascular necrosis of the femoral head and secondary OA

#### Clinical Scenario 9:
A 41-year-old female presents with headaches, visual disturbances, and nausea. Fundoscopy reveals the image shown.

Which of the following is the most likely underlying condition?
- **A)** Retinal Detachment
- **B)** Central Retinal Artery Occlusion
- **C)** Intracranial Hypertension
- **D)** Optic Neuritis
- **E)** Diabetic Retinopathy
- Explanation
- The image shows papilledema, which is often associated with increased intracranial pressure, as seen in intracranial hypertension. This condition can present with symptoms like headaches, visual disturbances, and nausea.
- Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (UH)
- Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) is a disorder of increased intracranial pressure in an alert and orientated patient. The most popular hypothesis is that IIH is a syndrome of reduced cerebrospinal fluid absorption.
- IIH, also known as pseudotumor cerebri, is a disorder of increased intracranial pressure that occurs mainly in overweight women of childbearing years, often in the setting of weight cause is unknown. It is characterised by increased intracranial pressure and its associated signs and symptoms in an alert and orientated patient but without localising neurological findings

- female sex
- weight gain
- using medications - nalidixic acid, nitrofurantoin, ketoprofen or indometacin (in Bartter's syndrome), vitamin A intoxication, isotretinoin, all-trans retinoic acid, thyroid replacement therapy in hypothyroid children, lithium, and anabolic steroids (which may cause venous sinus thrombosis).
- associated causal diseases - such as meningitis, sequel to subarachnoid haemorrhage, middle ear or mastoid-otitic hydrocephalus), bilateral radical neck dissections, superior vena cava syndrome, or increased right heart pressure. IIH may also be associated with endocrine conditions such as Addison's disease, hypoparathyroidism, obesity, and corticosteroid withdrawal
- Clinical features
- visual field loss
- headache
- transient visual obscurations - Episodes of transient visual loss usually last for <30 seconds and are followed by full restoration of vision
- pulse-synchronous tinnitus
- Photophobia
- Retrobulbar pain optical disc swelling - Care must be taken to differentiate true papilloedema from pseudopapilloedema. Optic disc oedema is the cause of the loss of vision in IIH.
- decreased visual acuity

- Investigation
- visual field testing (perimetry) — reveals visual field defects; enlarged blind spot, inferonasal loss, other nerve fibre bundle defects, or constriction of the field.
- dilated fundoscopy — shows papilloedema. Frisén grading stages papilloedema from stage O (normal optical disc) to stage 5 (severe). Grades 3 and greater are risk factors for poor visual outcome.
- visual acuity test - usually normal, or near normal, in patients with papilloedema, except when the condition is long-standing and severe, or if there is a neurosensory detachment. MRI of brain with or without contrast
- lumbar puncture at spinal 1-3/1.4
- other investigations to be considered
- magnetic resonance venogram of head
- optical coherence tomography
- Management
- Manage-the-rsik-fagtprs-that-may-ingr.ease-intragraniaLpressur.e
- In overweight patients - a weight-reduction programme can be instituted in overweight patients.
- other presumed causal factors - should be eliminated or treated (e.g., discontinue tetracyclines, retinoids, or excessive vitamin A; or, for corticosteroid withdrawal, restart corticosteroid therapy and taper more slowly).
- Patients are advised to follow a low-sodium diet and to only drink when thirsty (instead of forcing water intake).
- Prug-therapy

- Acetazolamide - 500 mg orally twice daily initially, increase by 250 mg/day increments every 4 days according to response, maximum 4000 mg/day.
- Furosemide - 20-40 mg orally two to three times daily
- Acetazolamide can also be given with furosemide in cases of refractory disease, but close monitoring of serum potassium is necessary.
- Analgesia for headache
- amitriptyline: 10-50 mg orally once daily at bedtime
- OR
- naproxen: 250-500 mg orally twice daily when required, maximum 1250 mg/day (maximum 2 days per week)
- alternative option, dihydroergotamine: 1 mg intravenously initially, repeat in 1 hour if no improvement, maximum 2 mg/total dose and 6 mg/week
- and
- metoclopramide: 5-10 mg intravenously every 8 hours when required for a maximum of 5 days, maximum 30 mg/day
- Consider cerebrospinal fluid shunting - Various shunting procedures have been used for intractable headache or failed medical therapy, including lumbar subarachnoid-peritoneal, cisternoatrial, ventriculoatrial, ventriculojugular, and ventriculoperitoneal shunts.
- Management of progressive visual loss - If patients lose vision in spite of full medical therapy, surgical treatment by optic nerve sheath fenestration or cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) shunting can be done. 10 / 10

