- Torsade de pointes here due to electrolyte imbalance: hypomagnesemia and hypocalcemia
- Hypocalcaemia secondary to hypomagnesemia
- Emergency treatment of torsade de pointes consists of removal of any torsadogenic stimulus and suppression of EADs(early after depolarization), which may include acceleration of heart rate to reduce the QT interval.
- Magnesium sulphate suppresses torsade by decreasing the influx of calcium ions, which in turn results in decreased amplitude of EADs. The initial dose is 2 g (20 ml of 10% solution), given intravenously over one to two minutes. This can be repeated up to a total of 6 g, with 5—15 minutes between doses; care should be taken to avoid magnesium toxicity.
- Initial treatment should also include the rapid correction of electrolytes including serum potassium concentrations, aiming for a target serum concentration of 5 mmol/l
- In some circumstances, urgent transvenous pacing may be necessary to suppress arrhythmias by increasing the baseline heart rate to 100 to

150 beats per minute. Temporary transcutaneous pacing has also been successfully used to treat torsade de pointes.
- If the patient is unstable :
- Immediate defibrillation - Technically the appropriate amount of energy is 2003 biphasic.

