#### Clinical Scenario 6:
A 24 year old female with history of fall brought into the ED by his father with complain of pain in the elbow. Her X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?
- **A)** Radioulnar dislocation
- **B)** Olecranon fracture C
- Radial head fracture
- **D)** Supracondylar fracture
- **E)** Humeral shaft fracture EXPIanation
- Suspect supracondylar fracture in children age 3- IO years, if X-ray shows Fat-pad sign or fracture.
- Suspect radial head fracture in adults, if X-ray shows Fat-pad sign or fracture
- Radial head/neck fracture
- Radial head fractures are the most common fractures of the elbow.
- They result from a fall on an outstretched hand.
- A specific associated injury, the Essex-Lopresti lesion occurs when there is disruption of the triangular fibrocartilage complex of the wrist and the interosseous membrane between the radius and ulna, causing dissociation of the distal radioulnar joint.

- Clinical features
- Pain the lateral elbow especially with pronation and supination of the arm
- there may be swelling laterally and tenderness with palpation of the radial head.
- Diagnosis
- Joint effusion (Fat pad sign) on x-ray
- abnormal displacement of the radiocapitellar line away from the center of the capitellum
- Management
- Nondisplaced fracture manage with sling immobilization, ice, elevation, analgesics, and referral to an orthopedic (fracture clinic) within I week.
- displaced fractures or those with restricted range of motion need surgical repair. Therefore, orthopedic referral should be made within 24 hours.

