- Consider Methaemoglobinaemia diagnosis in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration.

CO poisoning Carbon monoxide is a colourless, odourless, tasteless gas produced by incomplete combustion of fuels — this occurs when insufficient oxygen is present. Exposure to carbon monoxide causes tissue hypoxia, because carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin with about 240 times the affinity of oxygen and forms carboxyhaemoglobin.
- Exoosure to CO mav occur:
- Chimneys or flues are blocked
- lack of ventilation in the room
- Barbecues are used inside homes, caravans or tents
- Smoke in burning buildings Soot or yellow/brown staining around or on appliances. People are at risk of CO isonin
- Older people, children Women who are pregnant People with anaemia, Respiratory or cardiovascular disease
- Clinical features Headache, nausea & vomiting Vertigo, dizziness Cyanide poisoning Cyanide poisoning IS most often linked to house fires and combustion of products containing carbon and nitrogen. primarily foam rubber, wool, plastics, and other synthetic materials.
- CN disrupts the electron transport chain in the mitochondrial production of ATP by binding to and inhibiting cytochrome oxidase a.
- As aerobic cellular metabolism ceases, cells are forced into anaerobic metabolism With the subsequent generation of lactate
- Clinical features
- Neurologic symptoms - confusion, agitation, vertigo, headache, seizure,coma.
- Gastrointestinal - nausea, vomiting,
- Cardiac symptoms - chest pain, cardiac arrythmia Hypotension and bradycardia are common in cyanide poisoning
- pulmonary symptoms
- dyspnea tachypnae, pulmonary edema Bright red bleeding of mucous membranes
- Flushing of the skin Necroses in mouth and esophagus Bright red retinal vein on fundoscopic examination cherry red skin (very late and not often present), cyanosis Patient usually not cyanotic but maybe after prolonged respiratory failure and shock.
- Investiqations
- Arterial or Venous blood qas - Methemoglobinemia Methaemoglobinaemia is a disorder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemoglobin (MetHb) in the blood.
- Methaemoglobin in which iron (ferrous Fe2
- ) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3
- ). An abnormal MetHb level is any level > 1%
- Methemoglobinemia is either congenital or acquired
- Patients with methemoglobinemia will typically have brown blood
- Causes
- Benzocaine Dapsone
- Nitroglycerin
- Nitrofurantoin Nitroprusside Phenazopyridine
- Sodium Nitrite Amyl nitrite
- Contaminated well water
- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")
- Clinical features
- Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:
- < 10% - None (patients with underlying diseases may have mc.re symptoms at lower level) Slight discoloration (eg, pale, gray, blue) of the skin.
- Chocolate brown colored blood
- 20-30% - Anxiety, headache, tachycardia, lightheadedness
- Dyspnea, weakness, confusion, chest pain
- 50-70% - Arrhythmias; altered mental status. delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis
- > 70% - Usually, death Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is > 1.5 gld
- Investiqations VBG or ABG (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in conscicmsness— toxicitv is

Dyspnoea, pain (in chest or stomach)
- sleep changes, emotional lability
- delayed neuropsychiatric features (memory impairment, disorientation, irritability, inability to concentrate, personality change, emotional lability, neuropathy, incontinence,)
