- Explanation
- The ideal target might vary based on clinical circumstances, 120 mmHg is the upper limit of this target range and is considered acceptable in most cases to ensure adequate perfusion while avoiding excessive strain on the aneurysm.
- The European Society for Vascular Surgery recommends aiming for a target systolic blood pressure (SBP) of 70 to 90 mmHg. In practice, this target SBP is a good approach if definitive surgical repair is imminent.
- However, the Royal College of Emergency Medicine recommends a higher target SBP Of 90 to 120 mmHg. The previously recomrnended lower range of systolic BP of 70mmHg may be too low, particularly in the elderly. Blood products may be required but cross-matching must not delay transfer. Rarely, inotropic support may be required if the patient is not fluid responsive.

