#### Clinical Scenario 8:
An 18-year-old boy presented to the ED with mallet finger deformity. Which of the following is the initial management of this condition?
- **A)** Steroids
- **B)** Ice packing
- **C)** Mallet finger splint
- **D)** Refer to Orthopedic
- **E)** IV antibiotics Explanation
- Mallet finger

- MOI: It occurs due to forced flexion of the DIP while the joint is in extension. It is the most common tendon injury in athletes.
- There will be loss of full active extension at the DIP].
- Order x-ray to exclude associated injuries
- Management
- Immobilize DIP joint in a mallet splint with continuous full extension for 6 to 8 weeks. No flexion of the DIP joint is permitted for the duration of splinting. Thus, instruct patients to not take off the splint.
- If mallet finger with large fragment of fracture require referral to orthopedic for internal fixation untreated mallet finger may result in a swan neck deformity.

