#### Clinical Scenario 2:
A mother presents with an 18hours old baby in the ED with worsening jaundice who has been discharged 8 hours ago. On examination, his observations are within normal limit except for marked jaundice. What would be your management plan?
- **A)** Reassurance & discharge
- **B)** Admit for further assessment
- **C)** Refer tp paeditric surgeon
- **D)** Do heel prick test
- **E)** Temporary breastfeeding cessation EXPIanation;
- Arrange urgent admission to a neonatal or paediatric unit-within 2 hours if jaundice first appears at less than 24 hours of age. Jaundice
- Jaundice(also known as icterus) describes the yellow pigmentation of the skin, sclera, and mucous membranes resulting from raised plasma bilirubin.
- Normal plasma bilirubin levels are below 21 micromol/L.
- Clinical jaundice may not become apparent until serum bilirubin is greater than 51 micromol/L.

Breastmilk jaundice
- It is prolongation of physiological jaundice in breastfed babies.
- It usually presents within the first week of life (day 2—4) and peaks on day 7—15. In some cases, it can persist for up to 12 weeks. The mechanism is unknown, but possible reasons include a decreased caloric intake and inhibition of hepatic bilirubin excretion Same as in physiological factors Physiological jaundice
- It usually appears at 2 days of age, peaks on days 3—5, and then decreases, usually by around day 10 Physiological jaundice can occur in breastfed and formula-fed babies and results from increased bilirubin bad, decreased uptake by the liver, decreased conjugation in the liver, and decreased excretion into bile.
- Decreased gestational age/preterm delivery.
- Low infant birth weight.
- Development of jaundice within first 24 hours Of life.
- Male sex
- Visible bruising, Cephalhaematoma
- Maternal age older than 25 years.
- Maternal diabetes mellitus.
- Asian, European, or native American ethnicity.
- Sibling born with jaundice requiring phototherapy/other treatment.
- Dehydration.
- Poor caloric intakefincreased neonatal weight loss
- Breastfeeding Pathological jaundice
- it usually appears less than 24 hours.
- It can be caused bv a number of factors. including:
- most commonly Rhesus or ABO incompatibility.
- biliary atresia Sepsis
- Metabolic disorders (for example galactosaemia, hereditary fructose intolerance, alpha- 1 antitrypsin deficiency, hypothyroidism)
- Gilbert's syndrome and Crigler-Najjar syndrome
- G6PD deficiency. Same Manaqement of Physiological iaundice Reassurance & Advice leaflet Manaqement of Breastmilk iaundice First line Reassurance Temporary breast-feeding cessation and supplemental feeding maybe considered. and hydration. Third-line — exchange transfusion Manaqement of Patholoqical iaundice Arrange emerqencv admission - if there is jaundice with features of bilirubin encephalopathy (for example atypical sleepiness, poor feeding, irritability, vomiting, hypotonia followed by hypertonia).

