- **B)** Cyanide poisoning C' Methemoglobinemia
- **D)** Cocaine overdose D
- **E)** MDMA Explanation
- Patient clinical feature; cyanosed and low saturation Sp02 88% which does not improve with supplemental oxygen suggestive of methaemoglobinaemia. Methemoglobinemia
- Methaemoglobinaemia is a disorder characterised by a higher than normal level of methaemoglobin (MetHb) in the blood.
- Methaemoglobin in which iron (ferrous Fe2+) has been oxidized(ferric Fe3+). An abnormal MetHb level is any level > 1%
- Methemoglobinemia is either congenital or acquired
- Patients with methemoglobinemia will typically have brown blood . Causes
- Benzocaine . Dapsone
- Nitroglycerin
- Nitrofurantoin
- Nitroprusside
- Phenazopyridine

- Sodium Nitrite
- Amyl nitrite
- Contaminated well water
- Isobutyl Nitrite ("poppers")
- Clinical features
- Symptoms associated with higher levels of methemoglobin are as follows:
- < 10% - None (patients with underlying diseases may have more symptoms at lower level)
- 10-20% - Slight discoloration (eg, pale, gray, blue) of the skin.
- Chocolate brown colored blood
- 20-30% - Anxiety, headache, tachycardia, lightheadedness
- 30-50% - Dyspnea, weakness, confusion, chest pain
- 50-70% - Arrhythmias; altered mental status, delirium, seizures, coma; profound acidosis
- > 70% - Usually, death
- Cyanosis typically becomes apparent when total methemoglobin (total hemoglobin x % methemoglobin) is >1.5 g/dl
- Inyestigations
- VBG or ABG (both give same result for methemoglobin level) in a fresh sample of blood.
- Caution on estimating oxygen level - Routine pulse oximetry is misleading because methemoglobin absorbs light at the pulse oximeter, thus causing the pulse oximeter to falsely read around 85%.
- Pa02 readings on blood gases may be falsely elevated despite serious methemoglobinemia

- Management
- Remove exposures if possible.
- Supportive care (IV access, hydration or ventilation if necessary) or anyone who has a methemoglobin level
- Give methylene blue: 1-2 mg/kg (0.1-0.2 mL/kg of solution) as a slow push over 5 minutes
- Start with 1 mg/kg for mild symptoms and 2 mg/kg for more severe symptoms.
- Symptoms should resolve in minutes. If there is no response in 1 hour, repeat the dose or consider consider G6PD deficiency or an alternative diagnosis.
- Management in G6PD deficient patients
- Methylene blue may be ineffective or even cause hemolysis.
- Administration of an alternative reducing agent like vitamin C transfusion and hyperbaric oxygen. Persistent methemoglobinemia can be treated with blood transfusion
- Key_points
- Methemoglobinemia can result from exposure to a number of different medications. The most common are dapsone and topical anesthetic agents (i.e. benzocaine).
- Consider the diagnosis Methemoglobinemia in any patient with cyanosis and hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen administration.
- Administer methylene blue to any patient with abnormal vital signs, metabolic acidosis, end organ dysfunction or, a serum level > 25%

#### Clinical Scenario 2:
A 65-year-old brought into the ED with compliant palpitation and dizziness. His ECG is given. What would be your next step of management?
- **A)** DC cardioversion   B
- Beta blockers
- **C)** Adenosine
- **D)** Amiodarone
- **E)** Mgs04 Explanation
- ECG_finding;
- Normal sinus rhythm
- Non-sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a rate of slightly less than 300.
- Management — depends on clinical features, usually patients are asymptomatic, hence required reassurance and monitoring only. Symptomatic cause without electrolyte abnormality require beta blockers or calcium channel blockers therapy as the first line.
- Correct the electrolyte imbalance if electrolyte imbalance is the cause.
- ICD (implantable cardioverter defibrillator) if criteria match.

- Management of Non — sustained ventricular tachycardia
- Re-assurance + self-monitoring - NSVT is by definition a self-terminating NO cardiac comorbidity: Asymptomatic No cardiac comorbidity: Symptomatic Post-MI idiopathic dilated or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy event, and therefore usually no specific treatment is indicated Correct the electrolyte imbalance if electrolyte imbalance is the cause. Correct the electrolyte imbalance if electrolyte imbalance the cause. Symptomatic Without electrolvte abnormality:
- line - Beta-blockers (eg_, metoprolol, atenolol) or calcium-channel blockers (generally used when beta-blockers are contraindicated, e.g., asthma) are usually sufficient to control symptoms. line - Catheter ablation above ineffective
- 3«' line Antiarrhythmic agents such as flecainide or propafenone or amiodarone if above ineffective or who are not candidates for catheter
- With or Without heart failure Early re-perfusion + optimisation of medical therapy - Early re-perfusion decreases overall prevalence of NSVT following acute MI. Optimisation of medical therapy including beta-blockers. ACE inhibitors. antiplatelet therapy. and statins, reduces the risk of sudden cardiac death following acute MI
- Life style modification - Modifiable risk factors such as obesity and smoking should be addressed with a carefully planned exercise. diet, and weight loss programme.
- Consider ICD in heart failure patients. ICD is recommended for patients who are at least 40 days post-MI with class  heart failure, left ventricular EF (LVEF) 35% Or less,
- 1st line - implantable cardioverter defibrillator (ICD)
- ICD placement should be considered for patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and one Of the major risk factors for sudden cardiac death, including sudden cardiac arrest, spontaneous sustained VT, unexplained syncope, family history Of sudden cardiac death in a first-degree relative.
- It should be also implant in patient With
- If
- Implantable cardioverter defibrillator (ICD) is not indicated for patients With NSVT and idiopathic cardiomyopathy With good left ventricular function. Only Close observation and monitoring required.
- Note —for more detail check ventricular arrhythmia module in cardiology.

#### Clinical Scenario 3:
A 42-year-old female presents with a severely painful, red left eye and blurred vision. She wears contact lenses but denies any trauma. She is systemically well. An image is shown. After providing analgesia, what is the most appropriate next step in her management?
- **A)** Prescribe topical chloramphenicol and advise GP follow-up
- **B)** Oral acyclovir and self-referral to ophthalmology if no improvement
- **C)** Prescribe topical ganciclovir and steroid eye drops
- **D)** prescribe topical ganciclovir and self-referral to ophthalmology if no improvement
- **E)** Refer to ophthalmology for same-day review
- Contact lens wear is a well-known risk factor for bacterial keratitis, especially due to organisms like Pseudomonas aeruginosa.
- It can also predispose to fungal or Acanthamoeba keratitis, though these are less common.
- A dendritic ulcer is the hallmark of herpetic keratitis and would show a branching pattern with terminal bulbs under fluorescein staining. However, in above case (question) its not the branching pattern.

- Microbial keratitis (bacterial, fungal)
- Microbial keratitis is a potentially sight-threatening infection of the cornea. Bacterial cause
- The commonest bacterial corneal pathogens are:
- Pseudomonas sp. (Gram -ve)
- Staphylococcus sp. (Gram +ve)
- Streptococcus sp.(Cram +ve)
- other Gram -ve organisms
- Note: severe contact lens-related infections tend to be Cram -ve, particularly Pseudomonas species.
- EungaLgause
- Most common fungal corneal pathogens are:
- Candidasp. (yeast-like)
- Fusariumsp. (filamentous)
- Aspergillussp (filamentous)
- Predisposingfictors
- Bacterial keratitis is usually associated with one or more of the following:
- contact lens wear (incidence 4—5 times higher in CL wearers than non- wea rers)
- increased days Of wear
- overnight wear
- poor hand, lens and storage case hygiene
- youth
- male gender
- smoking
- internet purchase of lenses, particularly cosmetic lenses

- ocular surface disease, including:
- corneal exposure
- corneal decompensation
- chronic epithelial defect or recurrent corneal erosions
- neurotrophic keratopathy, e.g. secondary to HSK or diabetes
- ocular trauma or surgery, including loose or broken sutures
- Immune compromse
- topical steroid use
- lid margin infection (usually Staphyloccocal)
- Clinical features Svmptoms of microbial keratitis (bacterial. funqal)
- Usually unilateral
- Pain, moderate to severe (usually acute onset, rapid progression)
- Redness, photophobia (may be severe). watering/discharge, blurred vision (especially if lesion on or near visual axis)
- Awareness of white or yellow spot on cornea Siqns of microbial keratitis (bacterial. funqal)
- Lid oedema
- Epiphora
- Discharge (mucopurulent or purulent)
- Reduced visual acuity
- Conjunctival hyperaemia and infiltration
- Corneal lesion usually single (central or mid- peripheral) o o o o excavation of epithelium, Bowman's layer, stroma (ulcer resulting from tissue necrosis) stromal infiltration beneath lesion stromal oedema with folds in Descemet's membrane endothelial fibrin plaque beneath lesion Optical coherence tomography (OCT) may be helpful in determining depth of involvement

- Fungal keratitis produces similar signs to bacterial keratitis; however, it has been claimed that deep lesions, those having a feathery edge, raised profile, presence of satellite lesions and the presence of endothelial plaque are all features suggestive of a fungal as opposed to a bacterial infection. Hypopyon is seen in more than 50% of cases. Fungal keratitis may develop more slowly (however Fusarium infection can progress rapidly and invasively)
- ED management
- Cease contact lens wear - Warn contact lens wearers not to discard their lenses or lens cases, but to retain them if needed for culture
- Analgesia—if required
- Emergency (same day) referral is indicted if any of the following signs are present:
- infiltrate >lmm
- 2 or more adjacent lesions
- location 3mm or less from corneal centre
- AC reaction (210 cells in a Imm beam (2 1+ on the SUN scale)
- signs suggestive or fungal or acanthamoeba keratitis
- high likelihood of poor patient compliance to treatment
- These sight-threatening characteristics are indications for corneal scraping for microbiological Cram stain and culture, which should be performed prior to initiating antimicrobial therapy
- Eyedrops=Empirical treatment for lesions (<lmm) in the absence of the above clinical characteristics is based fluoroquinolone monotherapy e.g. gutt levofloxacin/moxifloxacin/ofloxacin hourly day and night for 48 hours, then every 2 hours daily for 72 hours, then every 4 hours for 7 days (The term "gutt therapy" refers to topical eye drop treatment). Monitor closely during this period and refer same day if not healing or if symptoms worsen.

- Fungal-keratitis-management;
- emergency (same day) referral to ophthalmologist without intervention if fungal keratitis is suspected,_after cease contact lens wear and analgesia.
- In summary_
- Bacterial keratitis:
- Correct to suspect in contact lens wearers who are not immunocompromised.
- However, bacterial keratitis should also be considered in any patient with corneal trauma, pre-existing ocular surface disease, or post- surgical complications. Contact lens-associated bacterial keratitis is frequently caused by organisms like Pseudomonas aeruginosa.
- Fungal keratitis:
- Correct to suspect in immunocompromised patients or those using steroids, but this is not exclusive.
- Fungal keratitis should also be suspected in cases of trauma with organic material (e.g., plant matter) or in warm, humid climates.
- Viral keratitis:
- It's true that viral keratitis (e.g., herpes simplex keratitis) can follow flu- like or cold symptoms.
- Additional features may include dendritic ulcers on fluorescein staining, recurrent episodes, or history Of herpes simplex infection

Keratitis Risk factors photoqraph Bacterial Contact lens uses, dry eye, abrasion Round/ white oval lesion (hypopyon) Viral History of nu or cold sores Fungal Trauma vegetative/outdoor, immunocompromised, steroid use Branching epithelial defect Infiltrates with feathery (dendritic (Grey-white lesion)

