- This is the case of acute on chronic respiratory acidosis
- ABG interpretation;
- 1st stepclt shows respiratory acidosis, bicarb slightly raised but has not developed appropriate metabolic compensation.
- 2nd step to check: is it acute or chronic?
- To use rule for acute: For every rise of 10mmHg/1.33kpa of the PaC02 above 40 mmHg, the bicarbonate will rise by Immol/L Therefore, bicarb should be elevated 5mmol/L to 12.1kpa/90mmHg PaC02, however, in this patient bicarb is elevated more than that its 8mmol/L. (it's not acute respiratory acidosis. Let's move to chronic)
- TO use 1 to IO rule for chronic: For every rise Of 10mmHg Of the PaC02 above 40mmHg, the bicarbonate will rise by 4mmol/L Therefore, bicarb should be elevated 20mmol/L to 12.1kpa/90mmHg PaC02. However, in this patient bicarb is elevated less than that which is 8mmol/L. is the case of acute on chronic respiratory acidosis (commonly seen in acute COPD exacerbation)
- Note; for more detail check ABC interpretation module in procedural & skills. You can find more cases discussion in the form of examples.

