#### Clinical Scenario 9:
A 45-year-old presented to the ED with a sensorineural loss in his left ear. Which Of the following statement is correct?
- **A)** Weber's test lateralizes to left ear
- **B)** patient can't hear whisper
- **C)** Rinne's test would be -ve
- **E)** BC>AC Explanation

Interpretation of Rinne & Weber tests Interpretation of Rinne & Weber tests 1 Normal Conductive hearing loss Sensorineural hearing loss Mixed hearing loss Rinne results
- AC>BC in both ears
- BC>AC in affected ear, (Rinne test —ve)
- AC>BC in unaffected ear (Rinne test +ve)
- AC>BC in both ears
- Unilateral (one ear) — in both affected and unaffected ear AC>BC and Rinne test would be
- BC>AC in affected ear, AC>BC in unaffected ear Weber results
- Midline
- Lateralize to affected ear 1
- Lateralize to unaffected ear, away from the affected ear 1
- Lateralize to unaffected ear, away from affected ear AC = Air conduction: BC = bone conduction

Weber test Normal Conductive loss, same ear Sensorineural loss, opposite ear Rinne test Air conduction (AC) Bone conduction (BC) AC > BC Normal BC > AC = Conductive loss
- Key_points:
- Weber test z tuning fork (512HZ) placed in middle of the forehead
- Rinne_testztuning fork placed on the mastoid bone and then adjacent to the outer ear
- Rinne test positive — (normal and sensorineural hearing loss)

- Rinne test negative — B>A ( conducting hearing loss)
- False negative— in severe sensorineural loss air conduction is absent, bone conduction present due to transcranial transmission of sound which is picked by the cochlea of good ear. Prevented by masking
- For example, if the left ear is completely dead, the sound waves travel to the good right ear on testing the bone conduction on the left. But the sounds are not heard when the tuning fork is held next to the external auditory meatus on the side being tested (left). In this case, the patient will state that bone conduction is louder than air conduction, giving a falsely negative Rinne test. 10 / 10 IO. A 54-year-old male presented to the ED with complain of dizziness and chest pain. His BP is 80/65mmhg. His ECG is given below What would be your next step of management?
- **A)** Adenosine
- **B)** DC cardioversion
- **C)** Vagal maneuver
- **D)** Bisoprolol
- **E)** Amiodarone Explanation

- ECG shows AVNRT, patient is unstable therefore DC shock should be given.
- Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is the most common form of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, or PSVT, in adults.
- AVNRT occurs when a reentrant circuit is present within the AV node itself. In this situation, there are two separate conduction pathways within the AV node instead of just one (present in about 5% of the general population).
- Stable patient management
- first line - Vagal maneuvers such as carotid massage or the Valsalva maneuver.
- Second line - adenosine 6mg then 12 and 18mg maximum 36mg (according to resus 0K) OR 6mg then 12mg and 12mg maximum 30mg (according to NICE)
- Third line - if above treatment ineffective then Beta blocker or rate limiting calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem
- fourth line - if above treatment ineffective then synchronized cardioversion. previous
