- **B)** Surgical chest drain at 2nd intercostal space on the right side
- **C)** Surgical chest drain at 5th intercostal space on the left side
- **D)** Surgical chest drain at 2nd intercostal space on the left side
- **E)** Needle thoracocentasis at 5th intercostal space midaxillary line Explanation Patient likely has developed post-operative complication, Chest tube
- placement following pulmonary lobectomy is common practice. Postoperative pleural effusion is drained and in case of an air leak, development of a (tension-) pneumothorax is prevented.
- prolonged air leak (>7 days): commonest complication post-lobectomy,
- up to 18% atelectasis: common
- pneumonia(rarely empyema)
- atrial fibrillation: common
- postoperative pulmonary edema
-
- ARDS bronchial dehiscence
- hemothorax: hemorrhage is common, 2.9% require 24 units packed
- cells chylothorax: more common with aggressive mediastinal nodal
- dissections bronchopleural fistula (BPF): very rare after lobectomy
