- Cancer-TNMstagingsystem
- It is the widest cancer staging system for doctors. It is used to stage malignant cancers that can spread to various parts of the body. The TNM staging system is the standard system of classification used by doctors, health centers, and hospitals that enables sharing of information and provides a better understanding of cancer to other doctors or to the patient itself.
- T- Tumor:The size of the primary tumor and its spread to surrounding tissues is analyzed. T values are assessed differently, depending on the structures involved. For example, Tl is used when there is a submucosal invasion, and T4 is used when there is an invasion of all layers of the colon.
- N- Nodal Involvement: This is used to determine the spread of cancer to the lymph nodes. Lymph nodes act as a filter when the tissue fluids enter lymphatic capillaries. N values given to a tumor are different depending on the regional lymphatic drainage and are tumor-specific.
- M- Metastasis:This is used to determine the spread of the tumor beyond regional lymph nodes.

T — Tumor
- Tx-unable to assess primary tumor TO — There is no evidence of primary lesion Tis — Carcinoma in situ
- Tl — Tumor penetrates submucosa and mucosa
- T2— The tumor invades but fail to penetrates the mucosal layer T3 — The tumor penetrates the subserosa
- T4— Tumors penetrates deep into the peritoneum or other organs N — Lymph Node NX — Unable to assess lymph node involvement
- NO — No lymph node involvement
- NI — There is metastasis in relevant organ/tissue nodes around 1 to 3 N2 — There is metastasis of four or more nodes.   M - Metastasis MX — Unable to assess distant metastasis MO — There is distant metastasis
- MI — There is distant metastasis
