#### Clinical Scenario 5:
You are tasked by a senior colleague to conduct research on the frequency of blood transfusions at your hospital. In addition, your senior asks you to include sensitive data, such as patients' religious beliefs, in your research report. Under the Data Protection Act 2018, which conduct might this request breach?
- **A)** Failure to inform data subjects Show drafts
- **B)** Incomplete data minimization
- **C)** Breach of confidentiality
- **D)** Data is not protected
- **E)** Processing special category data without lawful basis
- Explanation Under the Data Protection Act 2018 and the GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation), religious beliefs are classified as special category data (Article 9 of GDPR). This type of data requires explicit consent or a lawful basis for processing due to its sensitive nature. Including religious information in research without following the

necessary legal requirements would be a breach of the Data Protection Act 2018, particularly related to the lawful processing of special category data.
- Principles involved:

#### Clinical Scenario 1:
Special category data: Includes racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, genetic data, biometric data, and data concerning health or sexual orientation.

#### Clinical Scenario 2:
Lawfulness, fairness, and transparency: Processing must have a lawful basis, and individuals must be informed about how their data will be used.

#### Clinical Scenario 3:
Data minimization: Only data necessary for the purpose of research should be collected. Including sensitive data without justification violates this principle.
- Thus, in this case, including religious details in research without meeting GDPR conditions (explicit consent, or meeting specific lawful exceptions) would likely breach the Data Protection Act.
- Article 9 Processing of special categories of personal data

#### Clinical Scenario 1:
Processing of personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious or philosophical beliefs, or trade union membership, and the processing of genetic data, biometric data for the purpose of uniquely identifying a natural person, data concerning health or data concerning a natural person's sex life or sexual orientation shall be prohibited.

#### Clinical Scenario 2:
Paragraph I (mentioned above) shall not apply if one of the following applies:

- (a)the data subject has given explicit consent to the processing of those personal data for one or more specified purposes, except where [domestic law provides] that the prohibition referred to in paragraph I may not be lifted by the data subject;
- (b)processing is necessary for the purposes of carrying out the obligations and exercising specific rights of the controller or of the data subject in the field of employment and social security and social protection law in so far as it is authorised by [domestic law] or a collective agreement pursuant [to domestic law] providing for appropriate safeguards for the fundamental rights and the interests of the data subject;
- (c)processing is necessary to protect the vital interests of the data subject or of another natural person where the data subject is physically or legally incapable of giving consent; (d)processing is carried out in the course of its legitimate activities with appropriate safeguards by a foundation, association or any other not- for-profit body with a political, philosophical, religious or trade union aim and on condition that the processing relates solely to the members or to former members of the body or to persons who have regular contact with it in connection with its purposes and that the personal data are not disclosed outside that body without the consent of the data subjects;
- (e)processing relates to personal data which are manifestly made public by the data subject;
- (f)processing is necessary for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims or whenever courts are acting in their judicial capacity;
- (g)processing is necessary for reasons of substantial public interest, on the basis of [domestic law] which shall be proportionate to the aim

pursued and provide for suitable and specific measures to safeguard the fundamental rights and the interests of the data subject;
- (h)processing is necessary for the purposes of preventive or occupational medicine, for the assessment of the working capacity of the employee, medical diagnosis, the provision of health or social care or treatrnent or the management of health or social care systems and services on the basis of [domestic law] or pursuant to contract with a health professional and subject to the conditions and safeguards referred to in paragraph 3;
- (i)processing is necessary for reasons of public interest in the area of public health, such as protecting against serious cross-border threats to health or ensuring high standards of quality and safety of health care and of medicinal products or medical devices, on the basis of [domestic law] which provides for suitable and specific measures to safeguard the rights and freedoms of the data subject, in particular professional secrecy;
- (j)processing is necessary for archiving purposes in the public interest, scientific or historical research purposes or statistical purposes in accordance with Article 89(1) [(as supplemented by section 19 of the 2018 Act)] based on [domestic law] which shall be proportionate to the aim pursued and provide for suitable and specific measures to safeguard the fundamental rights and the interests of the data subject.
- 3.PersonaI data referred to in paragraph I may be processed for the purposes referred to in point (h) of paragraph 2 when those data are processed by or under the responsibility of a professional subject to the obligation of professional secrecy under [domestic law] or rules

established by national competent bodies or by another person also subject to an obligation of secrecy under [domestic law] or rules established by national competent bodies.
