- Patient with vasculitis
- Corticosteroid - The most common manifestation of vasculitis is skin rash, usually palpable purpura. Most patients have only a single lifetime episode of skin vasculitis. Treatment is a short course of corticosteroids.

- intravenous immunoglobulin (second line) - Intravenous immunoglobulin treatrnent in more severe/resistant cases may be effective, but therapeutic experience in vasculitis is limited.
- normal immunoglobulin human: 0.4 g/kg/day intravenously for 5 days
- Rituximab(third line) - Rituximab may be considered for patients with primary Sjogren syndrome with vasculitis or cryoglobulinaemia associated with vasculitis manifestations if they have experienced unacceptable adverse effects or toxicity with corticosteroids or other agents, or they are not able to taper and discontinue corticosteroid treatment.
- Patient with renal tubular acidosis
- A few patients may have severe manifestations of proximal renal tubular acidosis (ATA), even presenting with profound hypokalaemia. The usual therapy of type 2 RTA of any aetiology should be instituted. This includes potassium repletion and alkali by mouth:
- Patient with neuropathy_
- intravenous immunoglobulin - normal immunoglobulin human: 0.4 g/kg/day intravenously for 5 days. previous  Next
