- Analgesia - Ensure the patient has adequate pain relief
- VTE prophylaxis - Give venous thromboembolism (VTE) prophylaxis (enoxaparin) to all patients undergoing surgical repair of AAA

#### Clinical Scenario 5:
A 70-year-old elderly male with delirium was presented to ED with agitation. On initial assessment all his baseline reports are normal, after some time in ED he became more agitated. He is currently on furosemide and many other medications. What is the Cause of agitation?
- **A)** His daily drugs side effects
- **B)** Occult hip fracture
- **C)** Postural hypotension

O
- **D)** UTI
- **E)** Sub Dural Hematoma All baselines normal most likely cause of agitation and delirium would be drugs side effect in this. Delirium:
-
-
-
-
-
-
- Delirium(sometimes called 'acute confusional state')is an acute, fluctuating syndrome of encephalopathy causing disturbed consciousness, attention, cognition, and perception. Delirium usually develops over hours to days and behavioural disturbance, personality changes, and psychotic features may occur Delirium typically occurs in people with predisposing factors(such as advanced age or multiple comorbidities) when new precipitating factors(such as some medications or infection) are added. Classification Hyperactive delirium— may present with inappropriate behaviour, hallucinations, or agitation. Restlessness and wandering are common: Hypoactive delirium (more common)— may present with lethargy and reduced concentration and appetite. The person may appear quiet or withdrawn Mixed delirium— signs and symptoms of both hyperactive and hypoactive subtypes are present. Clinical features of delirium

- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:
- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.
- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.
- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.
- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.
- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).
- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep- cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night-time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal).
- Risk factors

Predisposing factors
- Older age (over 65 years). Cognitive impairment (such as dementia). Frailty/multiple comorbidities (such as stroke or heart failure). Significant injuries such as hip fracture.
- Functional impairment (for example immobility or the use of physical restraints such as cot sides).
- Iatrogenic events (such as bladder catheterization, polypharmacy, or surgery). History of, or current, alcohol excess.
- Sensory impairment (such as visual impairment or hearing loss). Poor nutrition. Lack of stimulation.
- Terminal phase of illness
- Clinical features Precipitating factors
- Infection - urinary tract infection, infected pressure sore, or pneumonia. Metabolic disturbance - hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia (including advanced carcinomatosis), or electrolyte abnormalities (including dehydration). Cardiovascular disorders- MI or heart failure. Respiratory disorders - pulmonary embolism or exacerbation COPD Neurological disorders - stroke, encephalitis, or subdural haematoma.
- Endocrine disorders - thyroid dysfunction or Cushing's syndrome. Urological disorders - urinary retention.
- Gastrointestinal disorders- hepatic failure, constipation or malnutrition.
- Severe uncontrolled pain. Alcohol intoxication or withdrawal Medications — antihistamine, antipsychotic, antidepressant, antispasmodics, NSAIDs , anti- hypertensive, corticosteroid. Psychosocial factors - depression, sleep deprivation, visual or hearing impairment, emotional stress, or change of environment
- Behavioural changes (develops acutely over hours to days) may includes:
- Altered cognitive function— the person may be disoriented, have memory and language impairment, worsened concentration, slow responses, and confusion.
- Inattention— the person may be easily distractible and have difficulty focusing and moving attention from one thing to another.
- Disorganised thinking— the person may have disorganised, rambling, or irrelevant conversation, unclear or illogical flow of ideas.

- Altered perception— the person may experience paranoid delusions, misperceptions, or visual or auditory hallucinations (in about 30% of people) that may be distressing.
- Altered social behaviour— the person may have intermittent and labile changes in mood and/or emotions (such as fear, paranoia, anxiety, depression, irritability, apathy, anger, or euphoria).
- Altered level of consciousness— the person may have a clouding of consciousness, reduced awareness of their surroundings, and sleep- cycle disturbances (such as daytime drowsiness, night-time insomnia, disturbed sleep, or complete sleep cycle reversal).
- Investigations
- Delirium is a clinical diagnosis based on a detailed history, examination, and relevant investigations
- Confirm a diagnosis of deliriumby carrying out a cognitive assessment based on the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5) criteria, the short Confusion Assessment Method (short-CAM), or the 4As test
- Arrange targeted investigations based on findings from the history and examination, for example:
- Urinalysis— to identify conditions such as infection or hyperglycaemia
- CXR or sputum culture- For chest infection
- FBC -for anaemia
- Management

- Most people with delirium should be admitted for urgent assessment, close monitoring, and treatment.
- If the person is deemed to not have capacity to consent, decisions should be made in the best interests of the person using the Mental Capacity Act 2005.
- If the person with delirium develops challenging behaviour (such as
- Address any underlying causes for the behaviour (such as discomfort, thirst, or need for the toilet).
- Advise moving the person to a safe, Iow-stimulation environment (such as a quiet room).
- Advise use of verbal and non-verbal de-escalation techniques (such as active listening, effective verbal responding, pictures, and symbols).
- If these measures fail, consider pharmacological measure and seek advice from an elderly care psychiatrist, or elderly care physician. Depending on the specific situation (for example the person is a danger to themselves or to others).
- NICE recommends - Short-term (for 1 week or less) low-dose haloperidol
- Antipsychotic drugs should be avoided, or used with caution in Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies
- Managemencprinciples in end-of-life care include:
- Considering non-pharmacological management of delirium in a person in the last days of life.

- Treating any reversible causes of delirium, for example psychological causes or certain metabolic disorders (for example renal failure or hyponatraemia).
- Considering a trial of an antipsychotic medicine (such as haloperidol or levomepromazine) to manage delirium, in the presence of intractable distress.
- Monitoring the benefits and side effects of these medicines at least daily and adjust the prescription as the clinical situation changes.

#### Clinical Scenario 6:
A 17-year-old male presents to the emergency department after being knocked unconscious during a boxing match. He was seen to have a brief loss of consciousness (<1 minute) and was confused upon waking. A CT scan of the head was performed and is reported as normal. He is now back to his baseline with no headache or other symptoms. He is keen to play in an important match in 2 weeks. What is the most appropriate advice to give his parents?
- **A)** He should stay away from all sports competitions for 21 days
- **B)** He should present to the hospital if he experiences a headache during any activity
- **C)** He can return to non-contact training imrnediately and full contact training after 1 week
- **D)** If he experiences any headache with activity, he should not return to full training for at least 2 weeks
- **E)** He can return to playing immediately as his CT scan is normal and he has returned to his baseline
- Explanation
- Current concussion management guidelines recommend a gradual return to play protocol following a concussion, even if symptoms have resolved and neuroimaging is normal. This typically involves a stepwise progression of activity with increasing physical and cognitive demands.
- Risk of Second Impact Syndrome: Returning to play too soon after a concussion significantly increases the risk of Second Impact Syndrome, a rare but potentially fatal condition where a second concussion occurs before the brain has fully recovered from the first.
- Concussion
- Concussion is a traumatic brain injury resulting in a disturbance of brain function. It affects the way a person thinks, feels and remembers things.
- Loss of consciousness (being 'knocked out') occurs in less than 10% of concussions and is not required to diagnose concussion. However,

anyone who loses consciousness because of a head injury has had a concussion.
- Concussion-can-affectpeop/e-in-four-main-areas Physical e.g. headaches, dizziness, vision changes
- Mood e.g. short tempered, sad, emotional
- Mental processing e.g. not thinking clearly, feeling slowed down
- Sleep e.g. not being able to sleep or sleeping too much
- Causes of concussion
- Concussion can be caused by a direct blow to the head but can also
- occur when knocks to other parts of the body result in rapid movernent of the head (e.g. whiplash type injuries). Concussions can happen at any age. However, children and
- adolescents may be more susceptible to concussion
- Initial assessment Red requiring urgent medical assessrnent t from an appropriate
- Healthcare Professional onsite or in a hospital Accident and Emergency (A&E) Department using emergency ambulance transfer if necessary; Any loss of consciousness because of the injury
- Deteriorating consciousness (more drowsy)
- Amnesia (no memory) for events before or after the injury
- Increasing confusion or irritability
