- Explanation
- The Trethowan sign is used to diagnose slipped upper femoral epiphysis. In this sign, the line of Klein passes above the femoral head instead of intersecting the femoral epiphysis as in the normal hip. It is best seen on both AP and lateral view.
- Slipped-upper-femoraLgpiphysis;
- It's a displacement of femoral epiphysis from metaphysis
- More common in children who are overweight and common in 10-15 years of age
- It may associated with endocrine pathology
- Clinical features:
- Pt will present with severe pain with externally rotated leg.
- There maybe shortening of the affected limb
- 'nyestigatipnzAP or frog-leg lateral view x-ray will reveal displacement of femoral head.
- The Trethowan sign is used to diagnose slipped upper femoral epiphysis. In this sign, the line of Klein passes above the femoral head instead of intersecting the femoral epiphysis as in the normal hip. It is best seen on both AP and lateral view.

- Management; Urgent evaluation and management is crucial to avoid further displacement and development of avascular necrosis.

#### Clinical Scenario 6:
A 40-year-old woman presents to the ED with palpitations. She has no other symptoms. She has been prescribed fluoxetine for psychiatric disorder and recently completed a course of erythromycin for chest infection. ECG is performed at triage. What is the most likely diagnosis?

A
- TOrsade de pointes
- **C)** Monomorphic VT
- **E)** Third degree AV block
- Explanation
- Macrolides cause QT prolongation.
- Current literature suggests fluoxetine has lowest risk . ongQT_syndrome
- Definition
- Long QT syndrome (LQTS) is a congenital or acquired condition that is characterized by a prolonged QT interval on the surface ECG and is associated with a high risk of sudden cardiac death due to ventricular tachyarrhythmias.
- In congenital LQTS, genetic mutations affect ion channels important in myocardial repolarization.
- Acquired LQTS may occur secondary to ingestion of QT interval- prolonging drugs, electrolyte imbalances, or bradyarrhythmias.
- Cause-of-AcquiredAQTS
- Drugs - including non-sedating antihistamines (such as terfenadine), r
- nacrolide antibiotics (such as clarithromycin and erythromycin),

antipsychotic drugs (such as risperidone), some antidepressant drugs (such as citalopram and escitalopram, tricyclics, and monoamine- oxidase inhibitors), and antifungals (such as itraconazole).
- Electrolyte imbalances - in particular hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and hypocalcemia.
- Bradyarrhythmias - any sudden bradycardia or atrioventricular (AV) nodal block may result in QT prolongation or pause-dependent QT prolongation.
- Central nervous system (CNS) lesions - such as intracranial hemorrhage (especially subarachnoid hemorrhage) and ischemic strokes.
- Malnutrition - liquid protein diet, starvation.
- Management-of-torsade-de-pointes
- Treat or remove the causative agents
- Correct electrolyte imbalance
- If patient has adverse features (hypotension, shock, etc) treat immediately with synchronized cardioversion
- Other all patients give intravenous magnesium sulphate 2g over 10 minutes
- Complications
- Torsades de pointes
- Cardiac arrest
- Sudden cardiac death

